WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.428 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.428 --> 00:00:01.928 align:middle line:90%


00:00:01.928 --> 00:00:03.832 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.832 --> 00:00:05.260 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:05.260 --> 00:00:08.942 align:middle line:90%


00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:10.400 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Today
we're going to talk

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:12.560 align:middle line:90%
about imperfect competition.

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:17.780 align:middle line:84%
I want to start with just a
small review of what we've done,

00:00:17.780 --> 00:00:19.700 align:middle line:84%
and then we'll move to
imperfect competition.

00:00:19.700 --> 00:00:21.158 align:middle line:84%
I think this fits
in with a broader

00:00:21.158 --> 00:00:24.480 align:middle line:84%
theme of the course where or a
broader approach in the course

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.880 align:middle line:84%
where we're going to start
with some simple examples

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:28.680 align:middle line:90%
to try to motivate the theory.

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:31.280 align:middle line:84%
Then we'll dig deep
into the theory

00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:33.360 align:middle line:84%
and maybe prove some
theorems, and then we'll

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:35.880 align:middle line:84%
apply the theory to some
substantive economic

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:36.497 align:middle line:90%
applications.

00:00:36.497 --> 00:00:38.080 align:middle line:84%
Today is going to
be one of those days

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.800 align:middle line:84%
where we dig into a substantive
economic application.

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.180 align:middle line:84%
So if you don't have much
of an econ background,

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.100 align:middle line:84%
some of what we covered
today may be new to you.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.820 align:middle line:84%
If you have take a
lot of econ courses,

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.120 align:middle line:90%
this may be a bit of a review.

00:00:50.120 --> 00:00:52.600 align:middle line:84%
But just a reminder of
what we've done so far,

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:55.440 align:middle line:84%
we've been studying
strategic form games,

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.090 align:middle line:84%
and we've looked at
various solution concepts.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.650 align:middle line:84%
And we've said that, in
a strategic form game,

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:06.050 align:middle line:84%
we applied this procedure of
iterated elimination of strictly

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.730 align:middle line:90%
dominated strategies.

00:01:07.730 --> 00:01:13.390 align:middle line:84%
And we generated these
nested sets of strategies.

00:01:13.390 --> 00:01:15.270 align:middle line:84%
And at the end, we
got to S infinity.

00:01:15.270 --> 00:01:18.090 align:middle line:84%
And remember, what we said
is that stronger assumptions

00:01:18.090 --> 00:01:20.610 align:middle line:84%
about rationality
and higher-order

00:01:20.610 --> 00:01:23.930 align:middle line:84%
knowledge of rationality allowed
us to get sharper predictions

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.250 align:middle line:90%
about how players will play.

00:01:26.250 --> 00:01:29.170 align:middle line:84%
And at the end, when
we get to S infinity,

00:01:29.170 --> 00:01:33.250 align:middle line:84%
what we further know
is that a subset

00:01:33.250 --> 00:01:36.130 align:middle line:84%
of the rationalizable
strategies are-- maybe

00:01:36.130 --> 00:01:38.250 align:middle line:84%
I'll jump down to what
we showed last time.

00:01:38.250 --> 00:01:45.130 align:middle line:84%
We showed these
two relationships.

00:01:45.130 --> 00:01:48.610 align:middle line:84%
So we showed that every
dominant strategy equilibrium

00:01:48.610 --> 00:01:51.610 align:middle line:84%
is a Nash equilibrium,
specifically a pure strategy

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.050 align:middle line:90%
Nash equilibrium.

00:01:53.050 --> 00:01:55.050 align:middle line:84%
And we showed that
every pure strategy Nash

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.730 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium is rationalizable.

00:01:58.730 --> 00:02:00.690 align:middle line:84%
But there's actually
one more inequality--

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.530 align:middle line:90%
or inclusion we can put here.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:06.250 align:middle line:84%
And here I'll put
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium

00:02:06.250 --> 00:02:09.050 align:middle line:90%
with positive probability.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.990 align:middle line:84%
So let me define
what I did here.

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.850 align:middle line:84%
And the proof of this inclusion
is quite similar to the proof

00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:18.210 align:middle line:84%
that we did in
class last Thursday.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.330 align:middle line:84%
So I'd encourage you to
go through the argument

00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:22.190 align:middle line:84%
and see if you can
extend it to this case.

00:02:22.190 --> 00:02:26.530 align:middle line:84%
So here what I mean is this is
the set of all strategy profiles

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:34.530 align:middle line:84%
that are played with
positive probability

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:37.270 align:middle line:84%
in some mixed-strategy
Nash equilibrium.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:52.050 align:middle line:90%


00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.070 align:middle line:84%
So just to make sure
we're on the same page,

00:02:54.070 --> 00:02:57.780 align:middle line:84%
why is it the case that every
pure strategy Nash equilibrium

00:02:57.780 --> 00:02:59.620 align:middle line:90%
is an element of this set?

00:02:59.620 --> 00:03:01.980 align:middle line:84%
Why is every pure
strategy Nash equilibrium

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:05.060 align:middle line:84%
in this set of positive
probability mixed-strategy Nash

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:06.320 align:middle line:90%
equilibrium profiles?

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:10.220 align:middle line:90%


00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:10.900 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:12.525 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I don't
remember [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:12.525 --> 00:03:14.000 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: That's fine, yeah.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:15.820 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
strategy [INAUDIBLE].

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:16.520 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Yeah, exactly.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:18.145 align:middle line:84%
Every pure strategy
and mixed strategy,

00:03:18.145 --> 00:03:19.240 align:middle line:90%
so that's the first step.

00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:21.180 align:middle line:84%
And then the second
step is to say, well,

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:24.140 align:middle line:84%
if something is a pure
strategy Nash equilibrium,

00:03:24.140 --> 00:03:27.260 align:middle line:84%
it's also a mixed-strategy Nash
equilibrium where everyone just

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.660 align:middle line:84%
plays their component
of that strategy profile

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:31.220 align:middle line:90%
with probability 1.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:34.100 align:middle line:84%
So since that's also a
mixed-strategy Nash equilibrium,

00:03:34.100 --> 00:03:37.540 align:middle line:84%
is this strategy profile played
with positive probability

00:03:37.540 --> 00:03:39.520 align:middle line:84%
in some mixed-strategy
Nash equilibrium?

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.260 align:middle line:84%
Yes, in fact, it's
played with probability 1

00:03:42.260 --> 00:03:44.560 align:middle line:84%
in a particular mixed-strategy
Nash equilibrium.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:47.360 align:middle line:84%
So it's clear that this
is a subset of this.

00:03:47.360 --> 00:03:49.380 align:middle line:84%
The argument that this
is a subset of this

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.143 align:middle line:90%
is maybe a little more involved.

00:03:51.143 --> 00:03:53.060 align:middle line:84%
But as I said, it's quite
similar to the proof

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:54.540 align:middle line:90%
we did last week.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:56.740 align:middle line:84%
OK, so that's the
theory up to the point

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.100 align:middle line:90%
that we've done so far.

00:03:58.100 --> 00:04:01.780 align:middle line:84%
And let me now move to the
main application for today,

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.680 align:middle line:90%
which is imperfect competition.

00:04:03.680 --> 00:04:13.340 align:middle line:90%


00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:15.422 align:middle line:84%
And to understand
imperfect competition,

00:04:15.422 --> 00:04:17.339 align:middle line:84%
I think it's helpful to
think of it as falling

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:19.420 align:middle line:90%
between two extreme cases.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:22.079 align:middle line:84%
So on the one hand, we
have perfect competition.

00:04:22.079 --> 00:04:28.980 align:middle line:90%


00:04:28.980 --> 00:04:32.040 align:middle line:84%
And then at the other
extreme, we have monopoly.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:34.660 align:middle line:90%


00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.760 align:middle line:84%
And then imperfect competition
falls somewhere in between.

00:04:37.760 --> 00:04:38.680 align:middle line:90%
So I'll put it here.

00:04:38.680 --> 00:04:46.500 align:middle line:90%


00:04:46.500 --> 00:04:50.720 align:middle line:84%
Now, formally defining these
concepts can be a little tricky,

00:04:50.720 --> 00:04:52.890 align:middle line:84%
but let me give some
ideas about what we mean.

00:04:52.890 --> 00:04:54.265 align:middle line:84%
And again, people
use these terms

00:04:54.265 --> 00:04:56.723 align:middle line:84%
in slightly different meanings,
in slightly different ways,

00:04:56.723 --> 00:04:57.830 align:middle line:90%
in different contexts.

00:04:57.830 --> 00:04:59.750 align:middle line:84%
But perfect
competition, we normally

00:04:59.750 --> 00:05:02.730 align:middle line:84%
think of that as a setting
where there's many small firms.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:08.630 align:middle line:90%


00:05:08.630 --> 00:05:11.370 align:middle line:84%
Monopoly is a situation
where we just have one firm.

00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.790 align:middle line:84%
They're the only firm offering
a particular good or product.

00:05:14.790 --> 00:05:18.408 align:middle line:90%
So here, we have one firm.

00:05:18.408 --> 00:05:19.950 align:middle line:84%
Usually, that one
firm would be large

00:05:19.950 --> 00:05:21.670 align:middle line:90%
since they're the only firm.

00:05:21.670 --> 00:05:24.690 align:middle line:84%
And then imperfect competition
lies somewhere in between.

00:05:24.690 --> 00:05:28.350 align:middle line:84%
We normally think that there
are maybe a few large firms.

00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:30.910 align:middle line:84%
This is sometimes
also called oligopoly.

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:34.250 align:middle line:90%
So a few maybe large firms.

00:05:34.250 --> 00:05:39.030 align:middle line:90%


00:05:39.030 --> 00:05:42.130 align:middle line:84%
And the implication of this is
that, in perfect competition,

00:05:42.130 --> 00:05:44.910 align:middle line:84%
when we have many
small firms, each firm

00:05:44.910 --> 00:05:46.570 align:middle line:90%
is what's called a price taker.

00:05:46.570 --> 00:05:51.590 align:middle line:90%


00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:52.690 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by that?

00:05:52.690 --> 00:05:54.650 align:middle line:90%
I'm some small firm in a market.

00:05:54.650 --> 00:05:58.190 align:middle line:84%
I can't influence what the
market price is for the product

00:05:58.190 --> 00:05:59.470 align:middle line:90%
that I'm selling.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.710 align:middle line:84%
So the classic
example of this would

00:06:01.710 --> 00:06:05.690 align:middle line:84%
be maybe commodities
markets, like wheat or corn.

00:06:05.690 --> 00:06:08.750 align:middle line:90%


00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:13.030 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm a small farmer and
I'm choosing how much corn

00:06:13.030 --> 00:06:15.870 align:middle line:84%
to produce or to sell
in the market this year,

00:06:15.870 --> 00:06:19.670 align:middle line:84%
my decision about how much
my particular farm produces

00:06:19.670 --> 00:06:23.190 align:middle line:84%
is unlikely to affect the
global market price for corn

00:06:23.190 --> 00:06:25.750 align:middle line:84%
because there's many, many
firms and many, many farms

00:06:25.750 --> 00:06:27.830 align:middle line:84%
in the world that
are producing corn.

00:06:27.830 --> 00:06:29.430 align:middle line:84%
And another important
aspect of this

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.930 align:middle line:84%
is that these are
homogeneous products,

00:06:31.930 --> 00:06:35.270 align:middle line:84%
and that's another
common attribute

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:37.470 align:middle line:90%
of perfect competition.

00:06:37.470 --> 00:06:40.070 align:middle line:84%
If we're all producing
corn, but my corn

00:06:40.070 --> 00:06:42.650 align:middle line:84%
is special and better
than everyone else's corn.

00:06:42.650 --> 00:06:45.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, I might not be such
a small player anymore.

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:48.000 align:middle line:90%


00:06:48.000 --> 00:06:50.260 align:middle line:84%
Then in the case of
imperfect competition,

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:52.480 align:middle line:84%
where we have a few
large firms, we normally

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:55.540 align:middle line:84%
think of what we say is that
these firms have market power.

00:06:55.540 --> 00:06:58.400 align:middle line:90%


00:06:58.400 --> 00:07:00.840 align:middle line:90%
What does market power mean?

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:02.873 align:middle line:84%
It's really the opposite
of being a price taker.

00:07:02.873 --> 00:07:05.040 align:middle line:84%
Though, we can think of
this kind of as a continuum.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:08.400 align:middle line:84%
With a price taker, I have
no control over the market

00:07:08.400 --> 00:07:10.480 align:middle line:90%
price for some item.

00:07:10.480 --> 00:07:13.800 align:middle line:84%
If I have market power,
then my production decisions

00:07:13.800 --> 00:07:16.800 align:middle line:84%
can have some effect on what
the market price will be.

00:07:16.800 --> 00:07:18.620 align:middle line:84%
So when we have a
few large firms,

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.560 align:middle line:84%
if someone floods the market
with a lot of their goods,

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:25.000 align:middle line:84%
then the market price
of that good may fall.

00:07:25.000 --> 00:07:26.960 align:middle line:84%
That's an example
of market power.

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:28.400 align:middle line:84%
So if there's a
few countries that

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:31.800 align:middle line:84%
produce oil in the world and
one of them, say Saudi Arabia,

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:34.180 align:middle line:84%
produces much, much
more oil this year,

00:07:34.180 --> 00:07:36.680 align:middle line:84%
then that's probably going to
have some effect on the market

00:07:36.680 --> 00:07:37.380 align:middle line:90%
price for oil.

00:07:37.380 --> 00:07:41.800 align:middle line:84%
It's probably going to bring
down the market price for oil.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:45.000 align:middle line:84%
When we have a few large firms,
maybe an example of this,

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:48.220 align:middle line:84%
there's different degrees
of imperfect competition.

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:53.380 align:middle line:84%
But maybe I think cell phone
companies are a good example.

00:07:53.380 --> 00:07:56.240 align:middle line:84%
In the US, you maybe have two or
three large cell phone carriers

00:07:56.240 --> 00:07:58.200 align:middle line:90%
that have their large networks.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:07:59.920 align:middle line:84%
But we don't have a
lot of small firms,

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:02.920 align:middle line:84%
and there's some
barriers to entry.

00:08:02.920 --> 00:08:07.920 align:middle line:84%
Another example of market
power could be due to location.

00:08:07.920 --> 00:08:10.120 align:middle line:84%
So it may be that
even if there's

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:12.560 align:middle line:84%
a lot of firms
producing a good, there

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:14.320 align:middle line:84%
may only be a small
number of firms

00:08:14.320 --> 00:08:17.040 align:middle line:90%
producing in a particular area.

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:18.960 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to
be really relevant when

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.320 align:middle line:90%
transportation costs are high.

00:08:21.320 --> 00:08:25.080 align:middle line:84%
So for instance,
maybe there's a lot

00:08:25.080 --> 00:08:27.960 align:middle line:84%
of coffee shops in the
world, but right around MIT,

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:29.600 align:middle line:90%
there may only be a few.

00:08:29.600 --> 00:08:31.780 align:middle line:84%
And if you take your
coffee over long distances,

00:08:31.780 --> 00:08:32.900 align:middle line:90%
it's going to get cold.

00:08:32.900 --> 00:08:35.340 align:middle line:84%
So you're not really considering
a coffee shop in New York

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.048 align:middle line:84%
even if New York,
maybe has better coffee

00:08:37.048 --> 00:08:39.120 align:middle line:90%
than some places in Boston.

00:08:39.120 --> 00:08:41.039 align:middle line:84%
Another source of
market power could

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:44.770 align:middle line:90%
be product differentiation.

00:08:44.770 --> 00:08:47.650 align:middle line:84%
And this is what I was getting
at with the corn example.

00:08:47.650 --> 00:08:49.250 align:middle line:84%
Corn is the opposite
case where we

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:50.970 align:middle line:90%
don't see much differentiation.

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:52.650 align:middle line:90%
But let's think about Apple.

00:08:52.650 --> 00:08:55.530 align:middle line:84%
There's a lot of different
companies that produce phones,

00:08:55.530 --> 00:09:00.010 align:middle line:84%
but you might find Apple iPhones
to be a different product

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:02.090 align:middle line:84%
than a Samsung phone
or a Google phone

00:09:02.090 --> 00:09:03.710 align:middle line:84%
or a phone produced
by another firm.

00:09:03.710 --> 00:09:05.570 align:middle line:84%
So even if there are
many companies that

00:09:05.570 --> 00:09:08.890 align:middle line:84%
are producing a smartphone,
if these companies

00:09:08.890 --> 00:09:10.968 align:middle line:84%
produce slightly
differentiated products,

00:09:10.968 --> 00:09:13.010 align:middle line:84%
then Apple has a bit of
flexibility in what price

00:09:13.010 --> 00:09:13.950 align:middle line:90%
it charges.

00:09:13.950 --> 00:09:16.937 align:middle line:84%
If an iPhone is a bit more
expensive than a Samsung phone,

00:09:16.937 --> 00:09:18.770 align:middle line:84%
a lot of people will
still choose that phone

00:09:18.770 --> 00:09:20.070 align:middle line:90%
because they like Apple more.

00:09:20.070 --> 00:09:22.730 align:middle line:84%
And the same with Samsung, there
may be some Samsung loyalists

00:09:22.730 --> 00:09:24.090 align:middle line:90%
out there.

00:09:24.090 --> 00:09:26.770 align:middle line:84%
And then monopoly is the
case, the extreme case

00:09:26.770 --> 00:09:28.690 align:middle line:90%
where we have a single firm.

00:09:28.690 --> 00:09:30.470 align:middle line:90%
So they still have market power.

00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:32.610 align:middle line:84%
Maybe they have
extreme market power.

00:09:32.610 --> 00:09:35.030 align:middle line:84%
It's always hard to find an
example of a pure monopoly,

00:09:35.030 --> 00:09:35.810 align:middle line:90%
but can you think of any?

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:37.310 align:middle line:84%
Are there any cases
that you've come

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:39.850 align:middle line:84%
across where you think there's
really only one firm that's

00:09:39.850 --> 00:09:42.810 align:middle line:84%
selling a particular good or
offering a certain service

00:09:42.810 --> 00:09:45.930 align:middle line:90%
or product?

00:09:45.930 --> 00:09:46.782 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:09:46.782 --> 00:09:47.490 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Subway.

00:09:47.490 --> 00:09:49.407 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: The subway is
a great example, yeah.

00:09:49.407 --> 00:09:51.690 align:middle line:90%


00:09:51.690 --> 00:09:56.850 align:middle line:84%
So the subway, say
the MBTA in Boston.

00:09:56.850 --> 00:09:58.562 align:middle line:90%
There's only one game in town.

00:09:58.562 --> 00:10:00.270 align:middle line:84%
You can't just, as a
private company say,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:01.978 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to start
digging up the streets

00:10:01.978 --> 00:10:03.850 align:middle line:84%
and building my
own subway system.

00:10:03.850 --> 00:10:06.230 align:middle line:84%
Now, of course, what
counts as a monopoly,

00:10:06.230 --> 00:10:08.730 align:middle line:84%
it always depends on what we
consider a close substitute.

00:10:08.730 --> 00:10:11.090 align:middle line:84%
So it is true that, instead
of taking the subway,

00:10:11.090 --> 00:10:13.630 align:middle line:84%
you could take a Bluebike,
or you could take a Lyft.

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:15.310 align:middle line:84%
That's some kind
of a substitute.

00:10:15.310 --> 00:10:17.210 align:middle line:84%
But in terms of
getting on a train that

00:10:17.210 --> 00:10:19.210 align:middle line:84%
goes quickly underground
and doesn't have

00:10:19.210 --> 00:10:20.770 align:middle line:84%
any contact with
traffic, that really

00:10:20.770 --> 00:10:24.050 align:middle line:84%
is something that only the
MBTA, only a government monopoly

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:25.310 align:middle line:90%
is able to provide.

00:10:25.310 --> 00:10:26.590 align:middle line:90%
What are some other examples?

00:10:26.590 --> 00:10:27.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:10:27.110 --> 00:10:28.790 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This is
more of a monopsony,

00:10:28.790 --> 00:10:31.110 align:middle line:84%
but if you're in
a coal mining town

00:10:31.110 --> 00:10:33.490 align:middle line:84%
and there's only
one mining company.

00:10:33.490 --> 00:10:36.373 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right,
that's a great example.

00:10:36.373 --> 00:10:38.290 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, maybe monopsony,
maybe I won't put it up

00:10:38.290 --> 00:10:39.830 align:middle line:84%
here just to avoid
confusion with monopsony.

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.163 align:middle line:84%
But that's the case where
you're the only buyer in town.

00:10:42.163 --> 00:10:45.020 align:middle line:90%
You're the consumer of labor.

00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:47.740 align:middle line:84%
Maybe, in that
context, if there's

00:10:47.740 --> 00:10:50.003 align:middle line:84%
a labor market in that town,
if there's a labor union,

00:10:50.003 --> 00:10:51.920 align:middle line:84%
that might be a monopoly
in that town as well.

00:10:51.920 --> 00:10:53.740 align:middle line:84%
But that's a great
example of a monopsony.

00:10:53.740 --> 00:10:54.550 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, in the back.

00:10:54.550 --> 00:10:56.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Normally,
like private companies

00:10:56.300 --> 00:10:59.420 align:middle line:84%
are contracted for power or
something by the government.

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.340 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yeah, so
often, utilities, and I

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:02.820 align:middle line:90%
put cell phones up here.

00:11:02.820 --> 00:11:04.440 align:middle line:90%
It's a close call.

00:11:04.440 --> 00:11:08.860 align:middle line:84%
But some utilities,
especially in a local market,

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:10.487 align:middle line:84%
if you have an
apartment here in Boston

00:11:10.487 --> 00:11:12.820 align:middle line:84%
and you try to find your
internet provider or your phone

00:11:12.820 --> 00:11:13.320 align:middle line:90%
provider--

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:15.720 align:middle line:84%
I guess, people don't have
landline phones anymore.

00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:17.460 align:middle line:84%
But your internet
provider, you may only

00:11:17.460 --> 00:11:19.348 align:middle line:90%
have one effective option.

00:11:19.348 --> 00:11:20.640 align:middle line:90%
So that's another good example.

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:21.080 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:11:21.080 --> 00:11:23.372 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I'm not sure if
that is imperfect competition

00:11:23.372 --> 00:11:26.380 align:middle line:84%
or monopoly, but pharmaceutical
companies and drugs they make.

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:28.775 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Great, so I
think, again, there's

00:11:28.775 --> 00:11:31.400 align:middle line:84%
sometimes blurry lines between
imperfect competition, monopoly.

00:11:31.400 --> 00:11:33.920 align:middle line:84%
But I think a great example
of this would be a patent.

00:11:33.920 --> 00:11:37.320 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm a private company
and I produce a drug,

00:11:37.320 --> 00:11:39.245 align:middle line:84%
I spend a lot of money
developing this drug,

00:11:39.245 --> 00:11:41.620 align:middle line:84%
I'm then given a certain period
of time by the government

00:11:41.620 --> 00:11:43.800 align:middle line:84%
where I'm the exclusive
supplier of that drug.

00:11:43.800 --> 00:11:47.157 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, whether a monopolist
or not might depend on,

00:11:47.157 --> 00:11:48.740 align:middle line:84%
well, is there a
very close substitute

00:11:48.740 --> 00:11:50.740 align:middle line:84%
for that drug that's
offered by another company?

00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:53.300 align:middle line:84%
But if I really come along
with a completely new drug,

00:11:53.300 --> 00:11:55.162 align:middle line:90%
I have a government monopoly.

00:11:55.162 --> 00:11:56.620 align:middle line:84%
The government
says, no one else is

00:11:56.620 --> 00:11:57.940 align:middle line:84%
allowed to produce
the drug that you're

00:11:57.940 --> 00:12:00.100 align:middle line:84%
producing for some period of
time, and the goal of that

00:12:00.100 --> 00:12:02.468 align:middle line:84%
is to give firms the incentive
to invest in these drugs

00:12:02.468 --> 00:12:04.260 align:middle line:84%
because developing them
is quite expensive.

00:12:04.260 --> 00:12:05.960 align:middle line:90%
Any final examples?

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:08.100 align:middle line:90%
These are all good.

00:12:08.100 --> 00:12:09.760 align:middle line:84%
I think another
big one is sports.

00:12:09.760 --> 00:12:13.260 align:middle line:84%
So the NFL, the MLB, there was
actually a Supreme Court ruling

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:16.540 align:middle line:84%
that gave the MLB an
official exemption

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:19.140 align:middle line:90%
from anti-monopoly laws.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:23.660 align:middle line:84%
So the NFL, we take it
for granted that there's

00:12:23.660 --> 00:12:25.460 align:middle line:90%
a draft for the NFL.

00:12:25.460 --> 00:12:27.140 align:middle line:84%
Why is there not a
draft when you guys

00:12:27.140 --> 00:12:29.820 align:middle line:84%
are trying to get jobs at tech
companies after you graduate?

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:31.500 align:middle line:84%
Well, they wouldn't all
participate in a draft.

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.680 align:middle line:84%
They're competing
against each other.

00:12:32.680 --> 00:12:34.222 align:middle line:84%
So the reason why
we can have a draft

00:12:34.222 --> 00:12:36.020 align:middle line:84%
is that it's legal
for these firms

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:39.570 align:middle line:84%
to band together and enter
this collective agreement

00:12:39.570 --> 00:12:41.590 align:middle line:84%
where they all engage
in a draft and they

00:12:41.590 --> 00:12:43.328 align:middle line:84%
agree to follow the
rules of this draft.

00:12:43.328 --> 00:12:45.870 align:middle line:84%
If Google and Facebook tried to
have a draft for MIT students

00:12:45.870 --> 00:12:47.950 align:middle line:84%
after you graduated and
have first-round picks

00:12:47.950 --> 00:12:50.710 align:middle line:84%
and second-round picks, that
would violate antitrust laws.

00:12:50.710 --> 00:12:53.670 align:middle line:84%
But the NFL and the
MLB, et cetera have

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:55.650 align:middle line:84%
gotten a special
government exemption,

00:12:55.650 --> 00:12:57.990 align:middle line:84%
and that allows them
to do various things,

00:12:57.990 --> 00:13:00.210 align:middle line:84%
bargain with broadcasters
in a collective way,

00:13:00.210 --> 00:13:02.065 align:middle line:84%
have things like a
draft, have restrictions

00:13:02.065 --> 00:13:04.190 align:middle line:84%
on the building of new
stadiums and the development

00:13:04.190 --> 00:13:05.350 align:middle line:90%
of new teams.

00:13:05.350 --> 00:13:07.540 align:middle line:84%
OK, so this is a bit
of actual economics,

00:13:07.540 --> 00:13:09.290 align:middle line:84%
maybe more applied
economics for us today.

00:13:09.290 --> 00:13:12.493 align:middle line:84%
But now let's move into some of
the classical theoretical models

00:13:12.493 --> 00:13:13.410 align:middle line:90%
that can explain this.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:15.190 align:middle line:84%
Maybe one more, I
guess, I had in my notes

00:13:15.190 --> 00:13:18.390 align:middle line:84%
that I didn't say would
be the US postal Service.

00:13:18.390 --> 00:13:20.170 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's a bit of
a partial monopoly.

00:13:20.170 --> 00:13:23.530 align:middle line:84%
There's very specific services
that no one else can provide,

00:13:23.530 --> 00:13:25.710 align:middle line:84%
but it does compete
with, say, UPS and FedEx

00:13:25.710 --> 00:13:26.890 align:middle line:90%
for certain other services.

00:13:26.890 --> 00:13:29.570 align:middle line:84%
So some of these things are
a bit-- are not so clear cut.

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:31.730 align:middle line:84%
And of course, when
firms go to court,

00:13:31.730 --> 00:13:33.470 align:middle line:84%
this is exactly what
they argue about.

00:13:33.470 --> 00:13:36.070 align:middle line:84%
So firms love to argue
that there are actually

00:13:36.070 --> 00:13:38.010 align:middle line:84%
a lot of close substitutes
for their products,

00:13:38.010 --> 00:13:39.910 align:middle line:84%
and they're not really a
monopolist in this small market

00:13:39.910 --> 00:13:42.452 align:middle line:84%
because really they're operating
in this much broader market.

00:13:42.452 --> 00:13:44.908 align:middle line:84%
So firms always want to argue
that the market they're in

00:13:44.908 --> 00:13:46.950 align:middle line:84%
is not some narrowly
defined market where they're

00:13:46.950 --> 00:13:49.792 align:middle line:84%
the only player but, in fact,
a much broader market where

00:13:49.792 --> 00:13:51.250 align:middle line:84%
there's other forms
of competition.

00:13:51.250 --> 00:13:52.470 align:middle line:84%
So if I were the
subway and I wanted

00:13:52.470 --> 00:13:53.890 align:middle line:84%
to say I'm not a
monopolist, I would say,

00:13:53.890 --> 00:13:55.432 align:middle line:84%
no, I'm in the
transportation market,

00:13:55.432 --> 00:13:57.270 align:middle line:84%
and there's tons of
forms of transportation

00:13:57.270 --> 00:13:59.170 align:middle line:84%
whereas if I wanted to argue
that they were monopolist,

00:13:59.170 --> 00:13:59.750 align:middle line:90%
I might say--

00:13:59.750 --> 00:14:04.590 align:middle line:84%
I might define transportation
a lot more narrowly.

00:14:04.590 --> 00:14:06.170 align:middle line:84%
So now that we have
that background,

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:09.990 align:middle line:84%
let's get into a few classical
game-theoretic models that

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:12.190 align:middle line:84%
try to understand the
interaction of firms

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:14.470 align:middle line:84%
in imperfect competition
and in monopoly.

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:16.830 align:middle line:84%
And the first ingredient
for these models

00:14:16.830 --> 00:14:20.228 align:middle line:84%
is going to be defining
what demand is.

00:14:20.228 --> 00:14:22.770 align:middle line:84%
So again, if you take an econ
class, you will have seen this.

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:25.310 align:middle line:84%
But demand captures
at a given price,

00:14:25.310 --> 00:14:28.510 align:middle line:84%
how many units of a product
am I willing to purchase?

00:14:28.510 --> 00:14:31.350 align:middle line:84%
And naturally, your
demand for a product

00:14:31.350 --> 00:14:32.950 align:middle line:90%
is going to depend on the price.

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:35.040 align:middle line:84%
Generally, people will
buy more of something

00:14:35.040 --> 00:14:37.647 align:middle line:84%
if the price of
that good is lower.

00:14:37.647 --> 00:14:39.480 align:middle line:84%
But let's go through a
really simple example

00:14:39.480 --> 00:14:41.317 align:middle line:84%
to try to make
this more concrete.

00:14:41.317 --> 00:14:43.900 align:middle line:84%
Let's think about-- let's say
it can be the demand for apples.

00:14:43.900 --> 00:14:45.020 align:middle line:90%
It doesn't really matter.

00:14:45.020 --> 00:14:47.562 align:middle line:84%
And let's say, in this economy,
there's only three consumers.

00:14:47.562 --> 00:14:51.000 align:middle line:84%
We'll call them consumer 1,
consumer 2, and consumer 3.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:56.238 align:middle line:84%
And apples, I would say, are not
an infinitely divisible good.

00:14:56.238 --> 00:14:57.780 align:middle line:84%
I mean, you could
sell half an Apple,

00:14:57.780 --> 00:14:58.780 align:middle line:90%
but that's really not done.

00:14:58.780 --> 00:15:00.040 align:middle line:84%
We're going to
think of these as--

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:02.290 align:middle line:84%
you're only going to be able
to buy 1, 2, or 3 apples.

00:15:02.290 --> 00:15:05.620 align:middle line:84%
You can't buy 1.75, or you
can't buy a lottery over apples.

00:15:05.620 --> 00:15:08.080 align:middle line:84%
Though, maybe you
could at some point.

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:10.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at
consumer 1, and let's say

00:15:10.040 --> 00:15:13.780 align:middle line:84%
that their valuation for the
first apple they get is $1.

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:16.600 align:middle line:84%
That's how much it's worth
to them to get an apple.

00:15:16.600 --> 00:15:18.360 align:middle line:90%
But they only want 1 Apple.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:20.480 align:middle line:84%
Once they get that
first apple, the value

00:15:20.480 --> 00:15:23.240 align:middle line:84%
they get from a second
Apple, the marginal value

00:15:23.240 --> 00:15:25.080 align:middle line:84%
of an additional
Apple drops to 0

00:15:25.080 --> 00:15:26.678 align:middle line:84%
because they're just
not that hungry.

00:15:26.678 --> 00:15:28.220 align:middle line:84%
And apples are
perishable, so they're

00:15:28.220 --> 00:15:30.280 align:middle line:84%
not-- it's going to
go bad by next week

00:15:30.280 --> 00:15:32.800 align:middle line:90%
when they want another apple.

00:15:32.800 --> 00:15:34.400 align:middle line:90%
Then we have consumer two.

00:15:34.400 --> 00:15:37.460 align:middle line:84%
They are similar to consumer 1
in that they only value 1 Apple,

00:15:37.460 --> 00:15:39.560 align:middle line:84%
but they value that
first Apple a bit more.

00:15:39.560 --> 00:15:42.080 align:middle line:84%
So for consumer 2, let's
say that first Apple

00:15:42.080 --> 00:15:44.360 align:middle line:90%
is worth $2 to them.

00:15:44.360 --> 00:15:46.220 align:middle line:84%
But again, they
only need 1 Apple,

00:15:46.220 --> 00:15:50.320 align:middle line:84%
so after that, their
value drops to 0.

00:15:50.320 --> 00:15:52.720 align:middle line:84%
And then consumer three
really loves apples.

00:15:52.720 --> 00:15:55.880 align:middle line:84%
And for them, the
first Apple is worth 3,

00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:58.960 align:middle line:84%
and they actually would even be
willing to get a second apple.

00:15:58.960 --> 00:16:00.760 align:middle line:84%
Once they have the
first, the second one

00:16:00.760 --> 00:16:03.800 align:middle line:84%
isn't quite as valuable, but
it does have positive value.

00:16:03.800 --> 00:16:06.760 align:middle line:84%
So that's going to be $1 for
the second apple and then 0

00:16:06.760 --> 00:16:07.300 align:middle line:90%
after that.

00:16:07.300 --> 00:16:09.200 align:middle line:84%
And after that, it's
going to keep being 0.

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:14.040 align:middle line:84%
They don't all of a sudden
want the 10th apple.

00:16:14.040 --> 00:16:18.920 align:middle line:84%
So once we understand the
individual consumers' demand,

00:16:18.920 --> 00:16:21.108 align:middle line:84%
we can put it together to
create a market demand,

00:16:21.108 --> 00:16:23.400 align:middle line:84%
but let's just make sure we
understand the individual's

00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:23.900 align:middle line:90%
demand.

00:16:23.900 --> 00:16:29.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's say I'm consumer 3,
and the price of apples is $4.

00:16:29.040 --> 00:16:30.710 align:middle line:90%
How many apples will I demand?

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:33.490 align:middle line:90%


00:16:33.490 --> 00:16:34.350 align:middle line:90%
None, right?

00:16:34.350 --> 00:16:36.990 align:middle line:84%
Because even this first
apple is not worth it to me,

00:16:36.990 --> 00:16:40.130 align:middle line:84%
so if the price is $4, I'm not
going to purchase any apples.

00:16:40.130 --> 00:16:42.410 align:middle line:84%
But if the price is
$2, then I'm going

00:16:42.410 --> 00:16:45.570 align:middle line:84%
to buy exactly 1 apple because
the first Apple is worth 3

00:16:45.570 --> 00:16:49.650 align:middle line:84%
to me, so it's worth it for me
to buy that first apple at $2.

00:16:49.650 --> 00:16:51.890 align:middle line:84%
But now, once I've
already bought 1 Apple,

00:16:51.890 --> 00:16:55.530 align:middle line:84%
the additional value I get
from a second Apple is only $1,

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:57.330 align:middle line:84%
but I'd have to
pay $2 to get it,

00:16:57.330 --> 00:16:59.690 align:middle line:84%
and therefore it's
not worth it for me.

00:16:59.690 --> 00:17:01.710 align:middle line:84%
So using each of
these individuals,

00:17:01.710 --> 00:17:04.470 align:middle line:84%
we can compute each of
these individual's demand.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:07.190 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to yield
an individual demand.

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:11.690 align:middle line:90%


00:17:11.690 --> 00:17:15.329 align:middle line:84%
But if I'm a firm and I'm
considering what price to charge

00:17:15.329 --> 00:17:18.730 align:middle line:84%
for my apples, I don't really
care about individual demand.

00:17:18.730 --> 00:17:20.810 align:middle line:84%
I don't care if James
is buying my apple.

00:17:20.810 --> 00:17:22.810 align:middle line:84%
I care about how many
people in the whole market

00:17:22.810 --> 00:17:24.150 align:middle line:90%
are going to buy my apples.

00:17:24.150 --> 00:17:27.190 align:middle line:84%
So what's relevant to the firm
is not the individual demand,

00:17:27.190 --> 00:17:28.710 align:middle line:84%
but what's called
the market demand.

00:17:28.710 --> 00:17:30.835 align:middle line:84%
And that's what we're going
to try to capture here.

00:17:30.835 --> 00:17:34.010 align:middle line:84%
So what is the market
demand for apples

00:17:34.010 --> 00:17:38.610 align:middle line:84%
in this very simple market
that only has three consumers?

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:40.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, what we're
going to do is we're

00:17:40.530 --> 00:17:43.890 align:middle line:84%
going to need to plot
the demand for apples

00:17:43.890 --> 00:17:45.850 align:middle line:90%
as a function of the price.

00:17:45.850 --> 00:17:48.010 align:middle line:84%
And there's a long
convention in economics

00:17:48.010 --> 00:17:50.690 align:middle line:84%
that we always put price
on the vertical axis.

00:17:50.690 --> 00:17:53.730 align:middle line:84%
So if you think of quantity
as a function of price,

00:17:53.730 --> 00:17:55.610 align:middle line:84%
that may feel kind
of backwards to you

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:59.930 align:middle line:84%
from the way it's done
in math, but this is just

00:17:59.930 --> 00:18:01.267 align:middle line:90%
a historical convention.

00:18:01.267 --> 00:18:03.350 align:middle line:84%
Some people argue that
there's good reason for it.

00:18:03.350 --> 00:18:05.230 align:middle line:84%
But we'll just take
it as a convention,

00:18:05.230 --> 00:18:07.230 align:middle line:84%
and we're always going
to write things this way.

00:18:07.230 --> 00:18:09.530 align:middle line:90%
So we have price and quantity.

00:18:09.530 --> 00:18:13.650 align:middle line:84%
And on the vertical axis,
let me make some marks.

00:18:13.650 --> 00:18:15.610 align:middle line:84%
By drawing won't be
perfect, but let's

00:18:15.610 --> 00:18:19.950 align:middle line:90%
say this is $1, $2, $3, $4, $5.

00:18:19.950 --> 00:18:22.930 align:middle line:90%


00:18:22.930 --> 00:18:26.580 align:middle line:84%
So what we want to do
is, for each price,

00:18:26.580 --> 00:18:29.620 align:middle line:84%
we want to compute what is
the total quantity demanded

00:18:29.620 --> 00:18:32.620 align:middle line:90%
at that price by the market?

00:18:32.620 --> 00:18:36.380 align:middle line:84%
So if the price is 5, what
is the total market demand

00:18:36.380 --> 00:18:37.240 align:middle line:90%
for apples?

00:18:37.240 --> 00:18:39.800 align:middle line:90%


00:18:39.800 --> 00:18:40.300 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 0.

00:18:40.300 --> 00:18:41.092 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: 0, right?

00:18:41.092 --> 00:18:42.500 align:middle line:90%
No one's willing to buy at 5.

00:18:42.500 --> 00:18:45.220 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, no one's
willing to buy at 4 either.

00:18:45.220 --> 00:18:48.000 align:middle line:84%
So if we start up
here, demand is just 0.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:50.080 align:middle line:84%
I'll try to make it
shaded so you can see it.

00:18:50.080 --> 00:18:51.160 align:middle line:90%
It's 0, 0, 0.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.180 align:middle line:90%
But then something happens at 3.

00:18:53.180 --> 00:18:59.140 align:middle line:84%
When the price drops down to 3,
well, now this person, player 3,

00:18:59.140 --> 00:19:01.800 align:middle line:84%
is willing to buy
exactly 1 apple.

00:19:01.800 --> 00:19:06.160 align:middle line:84%
So the market demand is going to
jump to 1 when the price hits 3.

00:19:06.160 --> 00:19:10.340 align:middle line:84%
And I should put
some marks here.

00:19:10.340 --> 00:19:14.780 align:middle line:84%
So here, maybe I'll put
it at a line like this,

00:19:14.780 --> 00:19:19.860 align:middle line:84%
it's going to jump up to 1
apple when the price hits 3.

00:19:19.860 --> 00:19:22.140 align:middle line:90%
And then it's going to stay.

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:26.460 align:middle line:84%
If the price is, say,
250, well, that's

00:19:26.460 --> 00:19:28.880 align:middle line:84%
going to make the third
consumer pretty happy.

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:30.860 align:middle line:84%
If the price is
now only 250, he's

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:34.040 align:middle line:84%
actually going to make a surplus
from this first purchase,

00:19:34.040 --> 00:19:36.460 align:middle line:84%
but it's not going to entice
him to buy another apple.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:38.940 align:middle line:84%
And it's also not going to
entice either of these players

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:39.960 align:middle line:90%
to buy an apple.

00:19:39.960 --> 00:19:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So the demand is
going to be constant

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:44.260 align:middle line:90%
until we hit a price of 2.

00:19:44.260 --> 00:19:48.900 align:middle line:84%
And then at a price of 2, how
many apples will be demanded?

00:19:48.900 --> 00:19:51.780 align:middle line:84%
Now 2 because the
third player is still

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:53.980 align:middle line:84%
going to demand an Apple,
but now the second player

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.580 align:middle line:84%
is going to demand
one apple as well.

00:19:56.580 --> 00:20:01.480 align:middle line:84%
So now we're going to have
this step again up to 2.

00:20:01.480 --> 00:20:02.980 align:middle line:84%
OK, I think you can
see the pattern,

00:20:02.980 --> 00:20:04.647 align:middle line:84%
but let's try to
understand what happens

00:20:04.647 --> 00:20:07.740 align:middle line:90%
when we get to a price of one.

00:20:07.740 --> 00:20:09.220 align:middle line:84%
Now what is demand
going to be when

00:20:09.220 --> 00:20:13.460 align:middle line:84%
the price gets to be 1 or
maybe a little bit below 1?

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:14.780 align:middle line:90%
Now it's 4.

00:20:14.780 --> 00:20:16.060 align:middle line:90%
And notice what happens.

00:20:16.060 --> 00:20:18.500 align:middle line:84%
When the price hits
1, the first player

00:20:18.500 --> 00:20:20.900 align:middle line:84%
is now willing to buy
1 apple, but that's not

00:20:20.900 --> 00:20:22.180 align:middle line:90%
the only thing that happens.

00:20:22.180 --> 00:20:25.310 align:middle line:84%
Now the third player is willing
to buy a second apple instead

00:20:25.310 --> 00:20:26.370 align:middle line:90%
of only 1 apple.

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.570 align:middle line:84%
+ now demand increases
for two reasons.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:31.190 align:middle line:84%
We have new consumers entering
the market choosing to buy

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:32.630 align:middle line:90%
who didn't previously buy.

00:20:32.630 --> 00:20:34.830 align:middle line:84%
And we also have
this apple lover

00:20:34.830 --> 00:20:37.470 align:middle line:84%
increasing the number of apples
that he buys from 1 apple

00:20:37.470 --> 00:20:38.630 align:middle line:90%
to 2 apples.

00:20:38.630 --> 00:20:42.390 align:middle line:84%
So now it's going
to jump up to 4,

00:20:42.390 --> 00:20:45.130 align:middle line:84%
and then it's going
to stay like this.

00:20:45.130 --> 00:20:49.510 align:middle line:84%
So we have some jumps
in the demand curve,

00:20:49.510 --> 00:20:52.270 align:middle line:90%
and it looks like this.

00:20:52.270 --> 00:20:53.950 align:middle line:90%
So that's our demand curve.

00:20:53.950 --> 00:20:56.910 align:middle line:84%
It looks kind of
jagged, and the reason

00:20:56.910 --> 00:21:00.190 align:middle line:84%
is that we're only considering
a market with three people.

00:21:00.190 --> 00:21:01.950 align:middle line:84%
But if I'm a supermarket
selling apples,

00:21:01.950 --> 00:21:04.770 align:middle line:84%
I'm, hopefully, selling to
more than just three people.

00:21:04.770 --> 00:21:06.670 align:middle line:84%
So in reality, we're
going to be aggregating

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.290 align:middle line:90%
a lot of these individuals.

00:21:08.290 --> 00:21:09.910 align:middle line:84%
And the more
individuals we aggregate

00:21:09.910 --> 00:21:11.730 align:middle line:84%
as we change the
scale of our graph,

00:21:11.730 --> 00:21:13.410 align:middle line:84%
it's going to get
smoother and smoother.

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:22.750 align:middle line:84%
So in our idealized model,
with maybe many consumers,

00:21:22.750 --> 00:21:27.678 align:middle line:84%
we're going to visualize this
as a continuous function.

00:21:27.678 --> 00:21:29.970 align:middle line:84%
Now, you might argue, well,
it's still really discrete.

00:21:29.970 --> 00:21:31.730 align:middle line:84%
Prices are discrete,
people are discrete.

00:21:31.730 --> 00:21:34.210 align:middle line:84%
But to make the
math a bit cleaner,

00:21:34.210 --> 00:21:35.990 align:middle line:84%
I think not much
is lost, and a lot

00:21:35.990 --> 00:21:37.990 align:middle line:84%
is actually gained in
terms of tractability

00:21:37.990 --> 00:21:39.410 align:middle line:90%
if we make it continuous.

00:21:39.410 --> 00:21:42.590 align:middle line:84%
So we might think of a demand
function that looks something

00:21:42.590 --> 00:21:46.870 align:middle line:90%
like this just as an example.

00:21:46.870 --> 00:21:49.190 align:middle line:84%
So again, the
interpretation is the same--

00:21:49.190 --> 00:21:52.110 align:middle line:84%
as the price goes down,
the total number of apples

00:21:52.110 --> 00:21:53.790 align:middle line:90%
demanded increases.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:56.010 align:middle line:84%
And again, it increases
for different reasons.

00:21:56.010 --> 00:21:58.350 align:middle line:84%
It can increase because
some consumers start

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.350 align:middle line:84%
buying who didn't
previously buy,

00:22:00.350 --> 00:22:04.070 align:middle line:84%
and other consumers increase the
number that they actually buy.

00:22:04.070 --> 00:22:06.870 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to call
this the demand function,

00:22:06.870 --> 00:22:15.030 align:middle line:84%
and we think of this
as a function Q of P.

00:22:15.030 --> 00:22:18.090 align:middle line:84%
So I always have to turn my
head when I think about this.

00:22:18.090 --> 00:22:20.360 align:middle line:84%
We have price on
the vertical axis.

00:22:20.360 --> 00:22:22.080 align:middle line:84%
We have demand,
the total quantity

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.400 align:middle line:90%
demanded on the horizontal axis.

00:22:24.400 --> 00:22:27.240 align:middle line:90%
And Q is a function of P.

00:22:27.240 --> 00:22:30.680 align:middle line:84%
Now, it's also sometimes helpful
to think of it the other way,

00:22:30.680 --> 00:22:35.580 align:middle line:84%
to think if I wanted a certain
number of apples to me demanded,

00:22:35.580 --> 00:22:38.200 align:middle line:84%
what is the price that I
would have to set in order

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:39.920 align:middle line:90%
to ensure that was the demand?

00:22:39.920 --> 00:22:43.160 align:middle line:84%
So instead of thinking of
the function from prices

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.920 align:middle line:84%
to quantities, we can
think of it the other way--

00:22:45.920 --> 00:22:48.720 align:middle line:90%
from quantities to prices.

00:22:48.720 --> 00:22:52.640 align:middle line:84%
And we sometimes
call this P of Q.

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:55.280 align:middle line:84%
And this is often called
the inverse demand function

00:22:55.280 --> 00:22:57.940 align:middle line:84%
because, mathematically,
this is just the inverse.

00:22:57.940 --> 00:23:01.440 align:middle line:84%
If we think of Q of P as
just a function, then P of Q

00:23:01.440 --> 00:23:03.780 align:middle line:84%
is literally the inverse
of that function.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:13.320 align:middle line:90%


00:23:13.320 --> 00:23:15.640 align:middle line:84%
So in problem sets and
when we set things up,

00:23:15.640 --> 00:23:18.200 align:middle line:84%
sometimes we'll
describe demand in terms

00:23:18.200 --> 00:23:21.320 align:middle line:84%
of the demand function, the
demand as a function of price.

00:23:21.320 --> 00:23:24.060 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes, we'll write
down the inverse demand,

00:23:24.060 --> 00:23:27.120 align:middle line:84%
which is the price as
a function of quantity.

00:23:27.120 --> 00:23:29.600 align:middle line:84%
These are just different
mathematical representations

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:31.400 align:middle line:84%
of exactly the
same object, which

00:23:31.400 --> 00:23:35.480 align:middle line:84%
is a correspondence between
prices and quantities.

00:23:35.480 --> 00:23:36.740 align:middle line:90%
Any questions on this?

00:23:36.740 --> 00:23:39.800 align:middle line:90%


00:23:39.800 --> 00:23:42.840 align:middle line:84%
So now that we've
set up demand, we'll

00:23:42.840 --> 00:23:44.420 align:middle line:90%
start with the monopoly problem.

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:45.962 align:middle line:84%
The monopoly problem
is a bit easier.

00:23:45.962 --> 00:23:47.587 align:middle line:84%
We don't really have
to do game theory.

00:23:47.587 --> 00:23:49.860 align:middle line:84%
And you've probably seen
this in other econ courses.

00:23:49.860 --> 00:23:51.277 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll
move to competition

00:23:51.277 --> 00:23:53.400 align:middle line:84%
where we really have
to take into account

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.060 align:middle line:90%
interaction between the firms.

00:23:55.060 --> 00:24:02.640 align:middle line:90%


00:24:02.640 --> 00:24:05.100 align:middle line:84%
So now let's look at
the monopoly problem.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:11.360 align:middle line:90%


00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:13.400 align:middle line:84%
So what we imagine
is I'm a monopolist.

00:24:13.400 --> 00:24:16.690 align:middle line:84%
I'm the only person
selling some good.

00:24:16.690 --> 00:24:18.490 align:middle line:84%
Say it's apples,
but usually, that's

00:24:18.490 --> 00:24:20.650 align:middle line:90%
not a good example of monopoly.

00:24:20.650 --> 00:24:22.650 align:middle line:84%
And what we're
going to assume is

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:33.410 align:middle line:84%
that the monopolist has some
constant marginal cost c, which

00:24:33.410 --> 00:24:36.290 align:middle line:90%
is greater than or equal to 0.

00:24:36.290 --> 00:24:38.710 align:middle line:84%
What do I mean by
constant marginal cost?

00:24:38.710 --> 00:24:42.170 align:middle line:84%
What I mean is if
the monopolist wants

00:24:42.170 --> 00:24:50.130 align:middle line:84%
to produce Q units of a good,
then the cost of those Q units

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:59.690 align:middle line:90%
is just c times Q.

00:24:59.690 --> 00:25:03.810 align:middle line:84%
So here, Q is just a
number, c is just a number.

00:25:03.810 --> 00:25:06.070 align:middle line:84%
We might think
that, at some point,

00:25:06.070 --> 00:25:08.670 align:middle line:84%
it becomes really costly to
produce an additional unit.

00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:11.850 align:middle line:84%
So often, we think that costs
in general, at some scales,

00:25:11.850 --> 00:25:15.913 align:middle line:84%
become convex eventually, that
if you have to really increase

00:25:15.913 --> 00:25:17.330 align:middle line:84%
production and
start hiring people

00:25:17.330 --> 00:25:19.370 align:middle line:84%
to work overtime and
plant the apples too

00:25:19.370 --> 00:25:21.830 align:middle line:84%
close to each other in the
farmland and all these things,

00:25:21.830 --> 00:25:23.152 align:middle line:90%
costs become convex.

00:25:23.152 --> 00:25:25.110 align:middle line:84%
That's probably realistic
in a lot of settings,

00:25:25.110 --> 00:25:27.930 align:middle line:84%
but let's stick to constant
marginal cost for now

00:25:27.930 --> 00:25:29.870 align:middle line:84%
over a certain
range of production.

00:25:29.870 --> 00:25:32.450 align:middle line:84%
That's probably
pretty realistic.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:35.025 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to
specify the demand.

00:25:35.025 --> 00:25:36.650 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand
here the difference

00:25:36.650 --> 00:25:46.170 align:middle line:84%
that these are assumptions about
the firm, which in this case

00:25:46.170 --> 00:25:47.850 align:middle line:90%
is a monopolist.

00:25:47.850 --> 00:25:50.250 align:middle line:84%
And now, when we write
down things about demand,

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:52.750 align:middle line:84%
we're not making assumptions
about the firm anymore.

00:25:52.750 --> 00:25:56.090 align:middle line:84%
We're making assumptions about
the population of consumers who

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:57.952 align:middle line:90%
are buying that firm's product.

00:25:57.952 --> 00:25:59.410 align:middle line:84%
So what we're going
to assume, just

00:25:59.410 --> 00:26:02.490 align:middle line:84%
to make the math
pretty easy, is we'll

00:26:02.490 --> 00:26:10.010 align:middle line:84%
assume that our demand
is Q of P equals--

00:26:10.010 --> 00:26:13.740 align:middle line:84%
well, we want to
just say 1 minus P,

00:26:13.740 --> 00:26:17.332 align:middle line:84%
so that as the price goes
up, the demand goes down.

00:26:17.332 --> 00:26:18.040 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:20.900 align:middle line:84%
People buy less when
the price is higher.

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:23.920 align:middle line:84%
But the problem is, well, if
the price gets really high,

00:26:23.920 --> 00:26:25.660 align:middle line:84%
then this demand
would be negative.

00:26:25.660 --> 00:26:27.120 align:middle line:90%
And that's a problem.

00:26:27.120 --> 00:26:28.960 align:middle line:84%
We don't think that
demand is ever negative.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:33.340 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to write
the maximum of 1 minus P

00:26:33.340 --> 00:26:38.020 align:middle line:84%
And 0 so that, once the price
gets high enough, specifically

00:26:38.020 --> 00:26:42.040 align:middle line:84%
when the price gets above 1,
demand is just going to be 0.

00:26:42.040 --> 00:26:43.640 align:middle line:84%
No one's willing to
buy at that price,

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:46.260 align:middle line:84%
but demand won't
actually become negative.

00:26:46.260 --> 00:26:49.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, when we solve
things, we often

00:26:49.340 --> 00:26:52.620 align:middle line:84%
kind of cheat a little bit,
and we forget about the max.

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:55.860 align:middle line:84%
And we hope that, at the
end, the prices we consider

00:26:55.860 --> 00:26:57.640 align:middle line:84%
won't be big enough
that this matters.

00:26:57.640 --> 00:27:01.185 align:middle line:84%
So what we'll often do is we'll
reason as if the max isn't here

00:27:01.185 --> 00:27:03.060 align:middle line:84%
and hope that at the
end, our answer involves

00:27:03.060 --> 00:27:04.660 align:middle line:90%
a price that's less than 1.

00:27:04.660 --> 00:27:07.000 align:middle line:84%
And if it does, that's
not going to be a problem.

00:27:07.000 --> 00:27:08.640 align:middle line:84%
But if we found a
price greater than 1,

00:27:08.640 --> 00:27:09.920 align:middle line:90%
then we'd have some issues.

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:11.820 align:middle line:84%
So that's just something
to keep in mind

00:27:11.820 --> 00:27:15.580 align:middle line:84%
when you're working with these
kinds of demand functions.

00:27:15.580 --> 00:27:23.580 align:middle line:84%
And let's remind us that this
is an assumption, well, not just

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:26.680 align:middle line:84%
about one consumer because
we have many, many consumers,

00:27:26.680 --> 00:27:29.120 align:middle line:84%
so it's assumption about
the population of consumers.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:35.820 align:middle line:90%


00:27:35.820 --> 00:27:38.700 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes hear about demand-side
and supply-side economics.

00:27:38.700 --> 00:27:41.380 align:middle line:84%
These are assumptions about
the supply side, the supplier

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.100 align:middle line:84%
of the good, and
these are assumptions

00:27:43.100 --> 00:27:47.380 align:middle line:84%
about the demand side, the
consumers who consume the good.

00:27:47.380 --> 00:27:49.393 align:middle line:84%
So now let's draw
this, and I want

00:27:49.393 --> 00:27:50.560 align:middle line:90%
to be a little more precise.

00:27:50.560 --> 00:27:52.400 align:middle line:84%
Not only is c greater
than or equal to 0,

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:55.780 align:middle line:84%
but let's actually say
it's between 0 and 1.

00:27:55.780 --> 00:27:57.780 align:middle line:84%
It's going to make
things a bit easier.

00:27:57.780 --> 00:28:01.100 align:middle line:84%
It could be exactly 0, but let's
say it's strictly positive,

00:28:01.100 --> 00:28:03.940 align:middle line:90%
and it's between 0 and 1.

00:28:03.940 --> 00:28:06.680 align:middle line:90%
So now let's draw this demand.

00:28:06.680 --> 00:28:09.750 align:middle line:90%


00:28:09.750 --> 00:28:13.510 align:middle line:84%
So as usual, I'm going to have
quantity on the horizontal axis

00:28:13.510 --> 00:28:15.330 align:middle line:90%
and price on the vertical axis.

00:28:15.330 --> 00:28:18.150 align:middle line:90%


00:28:18.150 --> 00:28:23.430 align:middle line:90%
And let me mark 1 and 1.

00:28:23.430 --> 00:28:25.810 align:middle line:84%
Of course, this just
makes the numbers easy,

00:28:25.810 --> 00:28:28.330 align:middle line:84%
but if you want to think of
this depending on the product,

00:28:28.330 --> 00:28:31.810 align:middle line:84%
maybe this is $0.01 or
$1 or $100 or $1,000,

00:28:31.810 --> 00:28:33.090 align:middle line:90%
but we'll just call it 1.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:34.710 align:middle line:84%
And similarly,
maybe this is tons

00:28:34.710 --> 00:28:36.760 align:middle line:90%
of apples or bushels of apples.

00:28:36.760 --> 00:28:38.510 align:middle line:84%
It could be various
things, but we'll just

00:28:38.510 --> 00:28:41.510 align:middle line:90%
use these simple units here.

00:28:41.510 --> 00:28:43.010 align:middle line:84%
And what does this
demand look like?

00:28:43.010 --> 00:28:45.750 align:middle line:84%
Well, if the price
is greater than 1,

00:28:45.750 --> 00:28:48.110 align:middle line:84%
then no one's willing
to buy at all.

00:28:48.110 --> 00:28:54.470 align:middle line:84%
So the demand looks like
that until we get to 1.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:58.790 align:middle line:84%
If the price is exactly
1, then demand is 0.

00:28:58.790 --> 00:29:02.570 align:middle line:84%
And on the other hand, if the
price is 0, then demand is 1.

00:29:02.570 --> 00:29:05.070 align:middle line:84%
So we know that there's-- here's
another point on the demand

00:29:05.070 --> 00:29:06.070 align:middle line:90%
curve.

00:29:06.070 --> 00:29:08.170 align:middle line:84%
And because of the
form of our demand,

00:29:08.170 --> 00:29:10.095 align:middle line:84%
this is what's called
a linear demand.

00:29:10.095 --> 00:29:11.470 align:middle line:84%
So the demand
curve is just going

00:29:11.470 --> 00:29:14.510 align:middle line:84%
to be a straight line that
connects our point here

00:29:14.510 --> 00:29:15.950 align:middle line:90%
and our point here.

00:29:15.950 --> 00:29:19.110 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll draw it
something like this.

00:29:19.110 --> 00:29:23.710 align:middle line:84%
If my axes were straight,
this should be, I guess,

00:29:23.710 --> 00:29:24.810 align:middle line:90%
an isosceles triangle.

00:29:24.810 --> 00:29:27.750 align:middle line:84%
But maybe I haven't
done it quite right.

00:29:27.750 --> 00:29:34.710 align:middle line:84%
OK, and just so we understand,
at any price P here,

00:29:34.710 --> 00:29:38.530 align:middle line:84%
this value is Q of
P, let's understand.

00:29:38.530 --> 00:29:41.230 align:middle line:84%
So at this price here,
I walk horizontally

00:29:41.230 --> 00:29:44.210 align:middle line:84%
to the value of the function
Q of P. I come down,

00:29:44.210 --> 00:29:46.710 align:middle line:84%
and that's the value Q of P
here, which is, in this case,

00:29:46.710 --> 00:29:50.190 align:middle line:90%
is exactly 1 minus P.

00:29:50.190 --> 00:29:53.790 align:middle line:84%
So now I need to represent
one more thing on the graph.

00:29:53.790 --> 00:29:55.630 align:middle line:84%
So far, the graph
is only representing

00:29:55.630 --> 00:29:57.270 align:middle line:90%
the demand side of the market.

00:29:57.270 --> 00:30:00.130 align:middle line:84%
Now, I also want to
represent the firm's costs.

00:30:00.130 --> 00:30:05.900 align:middle line:84%
So let me put c here, and I'll
draw a horizontal line here.

00:30:05.900 --> 00:30:09.220 align:middle line:84%
So this is going to represent
the firm's cost of production.

00:30:09.220 --> 00:30:11.800 align:middle line:90%


00:30:11.800 --> 00:30:16.840 align:middle line:84%
And now let's understand, if the
firm were to set a price of P,

00:30:16.840 --> 00:30:18.340 align:middle line:90%
what would their profits be?

00:30:18.340 --> 00:30:21.640 align:middle line:90%
So let's try to write this down.

00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:24.320 align:middle line:84%
I want to understand
what is pi of P?

00:30:24.320 --> 00:30:26.920 align:middle line:84%
So this is-- suppose
I'm the monopolist.

00:30:26.920 --> 00:30:29.120 align:middle line:90%
I choose to set a price of p.

00:30:29.120 --> 00:30:30.900 align:middle line:90%
What are my profits going to be?

00:30:30.900 --> 00:30:32.340 align:middle line:90%
If I set this price of p?

00:30:32.340 --> 00:30:35.920 align:middle line:90%


00:30:35.920 --> 00:30:38.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's break it
down into two parts.

00:30:38.520 --> 00:30:41.840 align:middle line:84%
The first is my profit
per unit that I sell.

00:30:41.840 --> 00:30:45.440 align:middle line:84%
So for each unit that I sell,
I sell it at a price of p,

00:30:45.440 --> 00:30:48.120 align:middle line:84%
but I have to pay a
cost of c to produce it.

00:30:48.120 --> 00:30:53.140 align:middle line:84%
So my profit per
unit is p minus c.

00:30:53.140 --> 00:30:59.200 align:middle line:90%


00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.860 align:middle line:84%
But then the question is I'm
getting this profit per unit.

00:31:01.860 --> 00:31:03.720 align:middle line:84%
But how many units
do I actually sell?

00:31:03.720 --> 00:31:07.020 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be p
minus c times Q of p.

00:31:07.020 --> 00:31:11.640 align:middle line:90%


00:31:11.640 --> 00:31:13.480 align:middle line:84%
And now we see the
basic trade-off

00:31:13.480 --> 00:31:15.200 align:middle line:90%
that the monopolist faces.

00:31:15.200 --> 00:31:18.480 align:middle line:84%
What happens when the
monopolist raises their price?

00:31:18.480 --> 00:31:22.800 align:middle line:84%
They increase their
profit per unit.

00:31:22.800 --> 00:31:25.080 align:middle line:84%
If I increase the price of
apples, each apple I sell

00:31:25.080 --> 00:31:25.780 align:middle line:90%
is great for me.

00:31:25.780 --> 00:31:28.120 align:middle line:84%
I'm making a lot of money
on each apple I sell.

00:31:28.120 --> 00:31:30.600 align:middle line:84%
But when I raise
that price, I'm not

00:31:30.600 --> 00:31:32.080 align:middle line:90%
going to sell as many apples.

00:31:32.080 --> 00:31:34.440 align:middle line:84%
So the number of units
that I sell goes down.

00:31:34.440 --> 00:31:36.400 align:middle line:84%
And the basic trade-off
for the monopolist

00:31:36.400 --> 00:31:39.760 align:middle line:84%
is to balance these two
effects, the per-unit profits

00:31:39.760 --> 00:31:44.360 align:middle line:84%
against the total number
of units that are sold.

00:31:44.360 --> 00:31:49.260 align:middle line:84%
And let's write this, for
prices that are between 0 and 1,

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:52.060 align:middle line:84%
this is going to take
the form like this.

00:31:52.060 --> 00:31:58.000 align:middle line:84%
So in general, Q of p is the
maximum of 1 minus p and 0.

00:31:58.000 --> 00:32:00.650 align:middle line:84%
But as long as p is not
too big, then this is just

00:32:00.650 --> 00:32:02.290 align:middle line:90%
going to reduce to 1 minus p.

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:05.850 align:middle line:84%
And I can substitute
this in here.

00:32:05.850 --> 00:32:08.450 align:middle line:84%
Now we can nicely
see this graphically.

00:32:08.450 --> 00:32:11.130 align:middle line:84%
Graphically, if I
look at this price p,

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:19.410 align:middle line:84%
well, this is going to be
the amount that I sell.

00:32:19.410 --> 00:32:22.170 align:middle line:84%
And p minus c is
my profit per unit.

00:32:22.170 --> 00:32:28.850 align:middle line:84%
So what I see is I can
actually shade in my profit

00:32:28.850 --> 00:32:31.490 align:middle line:90%
as this square or rectangle.

00:32:31.490 --> 00:32:41.090 align:middle line:84%
So the shaded rectangle
equals profit.

00:32:41.090 --> 00:32:42.890 align:middle line:84%
Let's make sure
we understand it.

00:32:42.890 --> 00:32:45.010 align:middle line:84%
I could actually look
at the entire rectangle

00:32:45.010 --> 00:32:47.170 align:middle line:84%
all the way down to
the horizontal axis.

00:32:47.170 --> 00:32:50.930 align:middle line:84%
That would represent revenue
because that entire rectangle,

00:32:50.930 --> 00:32:57.630 align:middle line:84%
its area is its height, which
is the price, times its width,

00:32:57.630 --> 00:32:59.250 align:middle line:90%
which is the demand.

00:32:59.250 --> 00:33:02.570 align:middle line:84%
So the entire rectangle
tells me the price

00:33:02.570 --> 00:33:04.650 align:middle line:90%
times the amount that I sell.

00:33:04.650 --> 00:33:05.630 align:middle line:90%
That's revenue.

00:33:05.630 --> 00:33:07.690 align:middle line:84%
That's the amount
of money I take in.

00:33:07.690 --> 00:33:09.370 align:middle line:84%
But I'm not counting
this little part

00:33:09.370 --> 00:33:11.770 align:middle line:84%
of the rectangle in
my profit because this

00:33:11.770 --> 00:33:14.650 align:middle line:84%
is the cost I have to pay
to produce that amount.

00:33:14.650 --> 00:33:22.330 align:middle line:84%
So remember, profit is profit
which equals revenue minus cost,

00:33:22.330 --> 00:33:24.930 align:middle line:84%
revenue meaning the total
amount of money that I take

00:33:24.930 --> 00:33:27.770 align:middle line:84%
in and cost being
the amount that I

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:31.130 align:middle line:84%
have to pay in order to
produce the amount that I sell.

00:33:31.130 --> 00:33:33.510 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want to make
the graph too ugly,

00:33:33.510 --> 00:33:36.890 align:middle line:90%
but this would be the cost.

00:33:36.890 --> 00:33:39.610 align:middle line:84%
I have to pay a
cost of c per unit,

00:33:39.610 --> 00:33:43.930 align:middle line:84%
and then the width here is the
number of units that I sell.

00:33:43.930 --> 00:33:45.750 align:middle line:84%
So what is the
monopolist trying to do?

00:33:45.750 --> 00:33:47.330 align:middle line:84%
They're trying to
choose this price

00:33:47.330 --> 00:33:50.210 align:middle line:90%
p to maximize their profit.

00:33:50.210 --> 00:33:54.370 align:middle line:84%
That is, choose this price
p to make this rectangle--

00:33:54.370 --> 00:33:57.923 align:middle line:84%
to make the area of this
rectangle as large as possible.

00:33:57.923 --> 00:34:00.340 align:middle line:84%
Now, let's just understand
some extreme cases, some things

00:34:00.340 --> 00:34:01.580 align:middle line:90%
they wouldn't want to do.

00:34:01.580 --> 00:34:03.980 align:middle line:84%
What if they set
the price to be 1?

00:34:03.980 --> 00:34:06.260 align:middle line:90%
What would their profit be?

00:34:06.260 --> 00:34:06.960 align:middle line:90%
0, right?

00:34:06.960 --> 00:34:09.980 align:middle line:84%
If they set the price to
1, no one's going to buy.

00:34:09.980 --> 00:34:12.659 align:middle line:90%
What if they set the price to c?

00:34:12.659 --> 00:34:14.659 align:middle line:90%
Then their profit is also 0.

00:34:14.659 --> 00:34:16.219 align:middle line:84%
A lot of people
are going to buy,

00:34:16.219 --> 00:34:18.679 align:middle line:84%
but if they're charging
exactly what their cost is,

00:34:18.679 --> 00:34:19.940 align:middle line:90%
they're not making any money.

00:34:19.940 --> 00:34:22.260 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, if they
set a price below c,

00:34:22.260 --> 00:34:24.820 align:middle line:84%
they'd actually be losing money
because their revenue would

00:34:24.820 --> 00:34:26.940 align:middle line:90%
be smaller than their cost.

00:34:26.940 --> 00:34:28.820 align:middle line:84%
So it's pretty clear
that the price should

00:34:28.820 --> 00:34:31.168 align:middle line:90%
be somewhere between 1 and c.

00:34:31.168 --> 00:34:32.960 align:middle line:84%
And let's actually
figure out what that is.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:39.179 align:middle line:84%
So let's now find the
optimal monopoly price.

00:34:39.179 --> 00:34:41.520 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll call it
the monopoly problem.

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:45.802 align:middle line:90%


00:34:45.802 --> 00:34:47.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, what is the
monopolist doing?

00:34:47.260 --> 00:34:53.340 align:middle line:84%
They're maximizing
over the price p

00:34:53.340 --> 00:35:00.060 align:middle line:84%
of pi of p, which equals
p minus c times 1 minus p.

00:35:00.060 --> 00:35:02.040 align:middle line:84%
Now, again, you
could say I'm not--

00:35:02.040 --> 00:35:05.260 align:middle line:84%
this is not quite correct
because, technically,

00:35:05.260 --> 00:35:07.220 align:middle line:84%
for some prices
they might choose,

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:09.980 align:middle line:84%
this is not a
reflection of demand.

00:35:09.980 --> 00:35:13.780 align:middle line:84%
But this is the correct formula
for the reasonable range

00:35:13.780 --> 00:35:15.640 align:middle line:90%
of prices between 0 and 1.

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.700 align:middle line:84%
And if I check at the
end that the price I get

00:35:17.700 --> 00:35:20.360 align:middle line:84%
is between 0 and 1, then
everything's going to be OK.

00:35:20.360 --> 00:35:22.540 align:middle line:84%
But recognize that
I'm not literally

00:35:22.540 --> 00:35:25.420 align:middle line:84%
claiming that if my
price is 10, then

00:35:25.420 --> 00:35:28.220 align:middle line:84%
my profit is going
to be negative

00:35:28.220 --> 00:35:30.120 align:middle line:84%
because if I set a
price of 10, in fact,

00:35:30.120 --> 00:35:34.500 align:middle line:84%
my profit will be 0 because no
one's going to buy the good.

00:35:34.500 --> 00:35:36.960 align:middle line:84%
Well, how do we maximize a
single variable function?

00:35:36.960 --> 00:35:39.420 align:middle line:84%
We take derivatives
and set them to 0.

00:35:39.420 --> 00:35:42.480 align:middle line:84%
You can check that this is
going to be a concave function.

00:35:42.480 --> 00:35:44.020 align:middle line:84%
So if we set the
derivative to 0,

00:35:44.020 --> 00:35:46.460 align:middle line:84%
we'll actually
find the maximizer.

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:50.140 align:middle line:84%
And so the derivative
pi prime of p,

00:35:50.140 --> 00:35:53.150 align:middle line:84%
well, we're just going
to use the product rule.

00:35:53.150 --> 00:35:56.910 align:middle line:84%
So we take the derivative of
the first term times the second.

00:35:56.910 --> 00:35:59.230 align:middle line:84%
The derivative of
the first term is 1.

00:35:59.230 --> 00:36:01.210 align:middle line:84%
So we just multiply
that by the second term,

00:36:01.210 --> 00:36:03.910 align:middle line:90%
and we get 1 minus p.

00:36:03.910 --> 00:36:07.070 align:middle line:84%
Then we have the
first term p minus c

00:36:07.070 --> 00:36:09.670 align:middle line:84%
times the derivative of
the second term, which

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:11.350 align:middle line:90%
is negative 1.

00:36:11.350 --> 00:36:13.470 align:middle line:90%
So we get negative 1 here.

00:36:13.470 --> 00:36:17.170 align:middle line:84%
So we get 1 minus p plus
minus 1 times p minus c.

00:36:17.170 --> 00:36:19.230 align:middle line:90%
Let's try to simplify this.

00:36:19.230 --> 00:36:26.310 align:middle line:84%
Well, we're actually
going to get 1 plus c.

00:36:26.310 --> 00:36:27.550 align:middle line:90%
minus 2p.

00:36:27.550 --> 00:36:32.110 align:middle line:90%


00:36:32.110 --> 00:36:39.230 align:middle line:84%
So we want to set this to 0
and solve for 0 equals this.

00:36:39.230 --> 00:36:42.470 align:middle line:84%
We're going to pull the 2p to
the other side and divide by p.

00:36:42.470 --> 00:36:46.350 align:middle line:84%
And what we can see is that
the monopoly price p star

00:36:46.350 --> 00:36:49.110 align:middle line:90%
is going to be 1 plus c over 2.

00:36:49.110 --> 00:36:52.150 align:middle line:90%


00:36:52.150 --> 00:36:53.970 align:middle line:84%
So in fact, it
will be the price.

00:36:53.970 --> 00:36:56.430 align:middle line:84%
What is 1 plus c over
2, geometrically?

00:36:56.430 --> 00:37:00.070 align:middle line:84%
It's the midpoint
between 1 and c.

00:37:00.070 --> 00:37:03.470 align:middle line:84%
And if you draw this
correctly, the optimal price 1

00:37:03.470 --> 00:37:07.430 align:middle line:84%
plus c over 2 will, actually,
make this rectangle a square.

00:37:07.430 --> 00:37:10.190 align:middle line:84%
I won't try to show it here
because I think my picture isn't

00:37:10.190 --> 00:37:12.350 align:middle line:84%
exactly to scale,
but what you'll find

00:37:12.350 --> 00:37:16.830 align:middle line:90%
is that this will be a square.

00:37:16.830 --> 00:37:20.090 align:middle line:84%
And this is actually
a nice geometric fact.

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:21.825 align:middle line:84%
So you could do all
the calculus here.

00:37:21.825 --> 00:37:23.450 align:middle line:84%
I mean, it's not too
hard in this case,

00:37:23.450 --> 00:37:24.950 align:middle line:90%
but it sometimes gets messier.

00:37:24.950 --> 00:37:32.950 align:middle line:84%
One way to think about this is,
well, p minus c plus 1 minus p,

00:37:32.950 --> 00:37:35.150 align:middle line:90%
that turns out to be constant.

00:37:35.150 --> 00:37:37.350 align:middle line:84%
So what you're actually
being asked to do here

00:37:37.350 --> 00:37:41.230 align:middle line:84%
is to find a rectangle
of maximal area

00:37:41.230 --> 00:37:43.470 align:middle line:90%
given a fixed perimeter.

00:37:43.470 --> 00:37:45.590 align:middle line:84%
So this is just a reflection
of the geometric fact

00:37:45.590 --> 00:37:47.870 align:middle line:84%
that if I fix a
perimeter, and I ask

00:37:47.870 --> 00:37:50.460 align:middle line:84%
you to find the rectangle
of largest area,

00:37:50.460 --> 00:37:52.460 align:middle line:84%
given that perimeter,
it's going to be a square.

00:37:52.460 --> 00:37:56.040 align:middle line:84%
So that's actually why
we get a square here.

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:59.480 align:middle line:84%
But that's just a
fact to keep in mind.

00:37:59.480 --> 00:38:02.440 align:middle line:90%
So now we found p star.

00:38:02.440 --> 00:38:05.360 align:middle line:90%
Let's now find Q star.

00:38:05.360 --> 00:38:09.800 align:middle line:84%
So Q of p star is
going to be what?

00:38:09.800 --> 00:38:13.120 align:middle line:90%
Well, q of p is just 1 minus p.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:21.980 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to get 1
minus 1 plus c over 2.

00:38:21.980 --> 00:38:24.560 align:middle line:90%


00:38:24.560 --> 00:38:28.880 align:middle line:84%
And if we simplify that, we
can think of this as 2 over 2.

00:38:28.880 --> 00:38:33.880 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
get 1 minus c over 2.

00:38:33.880 --> 00:38:37.520 align:middle line:84%
So what this says is, it's going
to be optimal for the monopolist

00:38:37.520 --> 00:38:42.360 align:middle line:84%
to set a price of 1 plus c over
2, and when they set that price,

00:38:42.360 --> 00:38:44.120 align:middle line:84%
the number of units
they're going to sell

00:38:44.120 --> 00:38:45.480 align:middle line:90%
is 1 minus c over 2.

00:38:45.480 --> 00:38:48.235 align:middle line:90%


00:38:48.235 --> 00:38:49.860 align:middle line:84%
Now, the first thing
we should observe,

00:38:49.860 --> 00:38:52.760 align:middle line:84%
which I think makes
sense, is that the price

00:38:52.760 --> 00:38:55.160 align:middle line:84%
the monopolist finds
it optimal to charge

00:38:55.160 --> 00:38:57.905 align:middle line:84%
is increasing in their
production costs.

00:38:57.905 --> 00:38:58.780 align:middle line:90%
And this makes sense.

00:38:58.780 --> 00:39:00.322 align:middle line:84%
So whenever you
solve these problems,

00:39:00.322 --> 00:39:02.375 align:middle line:84%
I think it's good to go
through the intuition.

00:39:02.375 --> 00:39:04.000 align:middle line:84%
Does it make sense
that, when something

00:39:04.000 --> 00:39:07.220 align:middle line:84%
is more expensive to produce,
I charge a higher price?

00:39:07.220 --> 00:39:08.760 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that certainly
makes sense.

00:39:08.760 --> 00:39:11.440 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it's often
helpful to decompose

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:15.800 align:middle line:90%
this in a very special way.

00:39:15.800 --> 00:39:23.880 align:middle line:84%
So we can actually write this
as c plus 1 minus c over 2.

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:25.340 align:middle line:90%
Let's check that that agrees.

00:39:25.340 --> 00:39:28.720 align:middle line:90%
This is 1/2 plus 1/2 half of c.

00:39:28.720 --> 00:39:34.040 align:middle line:84%
And this is c minus c over
2, so we still have 1/2 c.

00:39:34.040 --> 00:39:35.100 align:middle line:90%
And then we have a 1/2.

00:39:35.100 --> 00:39:38.840 align:middle line:90%
So the algebra works out.

00:39:38.840 --> 00:39:42.200 align:middle line:84%
And it's helpful to decompose
it this way because c

00:39:42.200 --> 00:39:45.280 align:middle line:90%
is the cost of production.

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:47.490 align:middle line:90%
So this term has a name.

00:39:47.490 --> 00:39:53.290 align:middle line:84%
Has anyone heard what we
might call this term here?

00:39:53.290 --> 00:39:54.750 align:middle line:90%
This is often called the markup.

00:39:54.750 --> 00:39:55.917 align:middle line:90%
And you might talk about it.

00:39:55.917 --> 00:39:58.550 align:middle line:84%
You go to a store, say, wow,
that's a really high markup.

00:39:58.550 --> 00:40:00.675 align:middle line:84%
If you go to a restaurant,
I could buy this chicken

00:40:00.675 --> 00:40:02.270 align:middle line:84%
at a grocery store
for this price.

00:40:02.270 --> 00:40:03.670 align:middle line:84%
But this restaurant is
charging a lot more.

00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:04.420 align:middle line:90%
That's the markup.

00:40:04.420 --> 00:40:06.930 align:middle line:84%
It's the difference between
the price you're paying

00:40:06.930 --> 00:40:09.350 align:middle line:84%
and the cost that they're
actually paying to produce it.

00:40:09.350 --> 00:40:11.110 align:middle line:90%
So this is called the markup.

00:40:11.110 --> 00:40:13.850 align:middle line:90%


00:40:13.850 --> 00:40:16.770 align:middle line:84%
So we see that monopolists
charge a markup.

00:40:16.770 --> 00:40:20.270 align:middle line:84%
They don't charge a price equal
to their cost of production.

00:40:20.270 --> 00:40:22.530 align:middle line:84%
They charge a price
that's strictly higher

00:40:22.530 --> 00:40:23.933 align:middle line:90%
than their cost of production.

00:40:23.933 --> 00:40:25.850 align:middle line:84%
And that's because they're
taking advantage of

00:40:25.850 --> 00:40:29.330 align:middle line:84%
or exploiting their
monopoly power.

00:40:29.330 --> 00:40:30.690 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:40:30.690 --> 00:40:34.250 align:middle line:84%
So now let's move to
the actual game theory

00:40:34.250 --> 00:40:40.950 align:middle line:84%
and think about what happens
if we have multiple firms.

00:40:40.950 --> 00:40:50.290 align:middle line:90%


00:40:50.290 --> 00:40:52.790 align:middle line:84%
So now what if there
are multiple firms?

00:40:52.790 --> 00:41:06.210 align:middle line:90%


00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:10.610 align:middle line:84%
Well, we have to think, how are
these firms going to compete?

00:41:10.610 --> 00:41:12.650 align:middle line:84%
It's not so clear
what they're doing.

00:41:12.650 --> 00:41:15.650 align:middle line:84%
And we're actually going to
study two classical models

00:41:15.650 --> 00:41:17.570 align:middle line:90%
of how firms compete.

00:41:17.570 --> 00:41:23.230 align:middle line:84%
So one model is what's
called quantity competition.

00:41:23.230 --> 00:41:28.085 align:middle line:90%


00:41:28.085 --> 00:41:30.210 align:middle line:84%
And here, I think the model
you should have in mind

00:41:30.210 --> 00:41:31.390 align:middle line:90%
is you're a farmer.

00:41:31.390 --> 00:41:32.790 align:middle line:90%
You think of two farmers.

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:35.810 align:middle line:84%
They're choosing how many
apples to bring to the market.

00:41:35.810 --> 00:41:37.930 align:middle line:84%
And once they get to
the market, the price

00:41:37.930 --> 00:41:39.570 align:middle line:84%
that they can get at
the market depends

00:41:39.570 --> 00:41:41.810 align:middle line:84%
on how many apples people
bring to the market.

00:41:41.810 --> 00:41:45.020 align:middle line:84%
So here, in quantity
competition,

00:41:45.020 --> 00:41:46.560 align:middle line:90%
firms choose quantities.

00:41:46.560 --> 00:41:52.380 align:middle line:90%


00:41:52.380 --> 00:41:54.840 align:middle line:84%
And then the market
chooses the price.

00:41:54.840 --> 00:41:58.140 align:middle line:90%


00:41:58.140 --> 00:42:01.140 align:middle line:90%
Maybe sets price.

00:42:01.140 --> 00:42:03.780 align:middle line:84%
So this is the oil
example I gave earlier.

00:42:03.780 --> 00:42:06.260 align:middle line:84%
If the US-- say, I gave
Saudi Arabia before.

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:08.140 align:middle line:84%
Let's say the US,
if the US increases

00:42:08.140 --> 00:42:10.580 align:middle line:84%
its oil or its
natural gas production

00:42:10.580 --> 00:42:13.860 align:middle line:84%
and floods the market, that's
going to decrease the market

00:42:13.860 --> 00:42:15.300 align:middle line:90%
price of oil.

00:42:15.300 --> 00:42:17.020 align:middle line:84%
So the US isn't
just saying this is

00:42:17.020 --> 00:42:19.100 align:middle line:90%
the price of oil I'm charging.

00:42:19.100 --> 00:42:20.700 align:middle line:84%
They produce more
oil, and then they

00:42:20.700 --> 00:42:23.420 align:middle line:84%
have to take the price
that they're given.

00:42:23.420 --> 00:42:25.415 align:middle line:84%
So that's the model of
quantity competition.

00:42:25.415 --> 00:42:27.540 align:middle line:84%
The other model is maybe
the more traditional model

00:42:27.540 --> 00:42:31.900 align:middle line:84%
that you think of
price competition,

00:42:31.900 --> 00:42:34.520 align:middle line:84%
where, here, firms
choose prices.

00:42:34.520 --> 00:42:36.220 align:middle line:84%
You have two stores
side by side.

00:42:36.220 --> 00:42:37.780 align:middle line:84%
They each post a
sign that says, this

00:42:37.780 --> 00:42:41.380 align:middle line:84%
is how much it costs to
buy this item at our store.

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:44.880 align:middle line:90%
So here, firms choose prices.

00:42:44.880 --> 00:42:49.980 align:middle line:90%


00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:53.220 align:middle line:84%
And then those prices determine
how much consumers actually

00:42:53.220 --> 00:42:55.060 align:middle line:90%
buy from each store.

00:42:55.060 --> 00:42:58.500 align:middle line:84%
So then you could say
the market sets demand.

00:42:58.500 --> 00:43:01.140 align:middle line:90%
Or I'll say quantities--

00:43:01.140 --> 00:43:07.900 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll say the
market chooses demand--

00:43:07.900 --> 00:43:09.000 align:middle line:90%
or chooses quantity.

00:43:09.000 --> 00:43:11.860 align:middle line:90%


00:43:11.860 --> 00:43:13.143 align:middle line:90%
So I see two stores.

00:43:13.143 --> 00:43:15.560 align:middle line:84%
They each have a sign that
says how much they're charging.

00:43:15.560 --> 00:43:18.100 align:middle line:84%
And I choose which
store to go to.

00:43:18.100 --> 00:43:20.140 align:middle line:84%
So I think these are
two very natural ways

00:43:20.140 --> 00:43:23.108 align:middle line:90%
of thinking about competition.

00:43:23.108 --> 00:43:24.900 align:middle line:84%
We're going to find in
the next few minutes

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:27.300 align:middle line:84%
that these actually give
very different answers.

00:43:27.300 --> 00:43:31.060 align:middle line:84%
So I think there's
the math, which allows

00:43:31.060 --> 00:43:33.112 align:middle line:90%
us to analyze these two models.

00:43:33.112 --> 00:43:34.820 align:middle line:84%
And then I think the
job of the economist

00:43:34.820 --> 00:43:36.860 align:middle line:84%
is to try to figure out
which of these models

00:43:36.860 --> 00:43:39.990 align:middle line:84%
is more appropriate in
a particular situation.

00:43:39.990 --> 00:43:42.150 align:middle line:84%
Because they both
seem reasonable,

00:43:42.150 --> 00:43:43.822 align:middle line:84%
and I gave a few
examples of markets

00:43:43.822 --> 00:43:45.530 align:middle line:84%
that might fit one
better than the other,

00:43:45.530 --> 00:43:47.930 align:middle line:84%
but whenever we have two models
that both seem reasonable,

00:43:47.930 --> 00:43:49.050 align:middle line:84%
they give very
different results,

00:43:49.050 --> 00:43:51.508 align:middle line:84%
we really have to think hard
about which is the right model

00:43:51.508 --> 00:43:53.510 align:middle line:84%
to capture the situation
we have in mind.

00:43:53.510 --> 00:43:56.350 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out
this one came first.

00:43:56.350 --> 00:43:59.855 align:middle line:90%
This is a model by Cournot.

00:43:59.855 --> 00:44:01.230 align:middle line:84%
It's easy to
remember these dates

00:44:01.230 --> 00:44:02.810 align:middle line:90%
because they mirror each other.

00:44:02.810 --> 00:44:05.153 align:middle line:90%
This was in 1838.

00:44:05.153 --> 00:44:07.070 align:middle line:84%
And then, in this model,
the price competition

00:44:07.070 --> 00:44:10.920 align:middle line:84%
is by Bertrand, and
this is actually 1883.

00:44:10.920 --> 00:44:12.670 align:middle line:84%
I mean, the dates don't
matter, but I just

00:44:12.670 --> 00:44:16.430 align:middle line:84%
think it's fun that the
dates are like that.

00:44:16.430 --> 00:44:19.450 align:middle line:84%
And Cournot came along,
wrote down this model.

00:44:19.450 --> 00:44:23.530 align:middle line:84%
He was motivated by,
actually, spring water,

00:44:23.530 --> 00:44:26.695 align:middle line:84%
these two firms that were
sitting on a spring and were,

00:44:26.695 --> 00:44:29.070 align:middle line:84%
I don't know, bottling or I
don't know how water was sold

00:44:29.070 --> 00:44:32.510 align:middle line:84%
in the 1800s, putting water
into buckets and selling it.

00:44:32.510 --> 00:44:35.230 align:middle line:84%
And then Bertrand
wrote an angry article

00:44:35.230 --> 00:44:37.670 align:middle line:84%
a few years later saying,
this is a crazy way

00:44:37.670 --> 00:44:38.730 align:middle line:90%
to model competition.

00:44:38.730 --> 00:44:40.890 align:middle line:90%
Firms don't choose quantities.

00:44:40.890 --> 00:44:41.973 align:middle line:90%
They choose prices.

00:44:41.973 --> 00:44:44.390 align:middle line:84%
And today, modern industrial
organization economists still

00:44:44.390 --> 00:44:46.550 align:middle line:84%
think about different ways
of modeling competition

00:44:46.550 --> 00:44:48.210 align:middle line:84%
and what's the right
way to think about it.

00:44:48.210 --> 00:44:50.330 align:middle line:84%
There's no right way, but
in different situations,

00:44:50.330 --> 00:44:51.430 align:middle line:90%
which one is better.

00:44:51.430 --> 00:44:55.310 align:middle line:84%
OK, So let's go through
these formal models.

00:44:55.310 --> 00:44:56.730 align:middle line:90%
So we'll start with Cournot.

00:44:56.730 --> 00:44:58.810 align:middle line:90%
We'll go chronologically.

00:44:58.810 --> 00:45:08.950 align:middle line:90%


00:45:08.950 --> 00:45:11.310 align:middle line:84%
So this is sometimes known
as quantity competition.

00:45:11.310 --> 00:45:13.450 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes called
Cournot competition.

00:45:13.450 --> 00:45:14.490 align:middle line:90%
Those are synonymous.

00:45:14.490 --> 00:45:17.650 align:middle line:84%
So maybe here I'll call
it Cournot competition.

00:45:17.650 --> 00:45:22.190 align:middle line:90%


00:45:22.190 --> 00:45:23.410 align:middle line:90%
So what's the model?

00:45:23.410 --> 00:45:29.830 align:middle line:84%
We have two firms, and we'll
call those firms 1 and 2.

00:45:29.830 --> 00:45:32.950 align:middle line:84%
And each firm has the same
constant marginal cost

00:45:32.950 --> 00:45:34.960 align:middle line:90%
of production.

00:45:34.960 --> 00:45:46.580 align:middle line:84%
So we'll say constant
marginal cost c at both firms.

00:45:46.580 --> 00:45:50.320 align:middle line:90%


00:45:50.320 --> 00:45:53.740 align:middle line:84%
And let's assume c is between
0 and 1, just as we did before.

00:45:53.740 --> 00:45:57.920 align:middle line:90%


00:45:57.920 --> 00:46:01.153 align:middle line:90%
And now we have market demand.

00:46:01.153 --> 00:46:03.320 align:middle line:84%
We're actually going to
look at the inverse demand--

00:46:03.320 --> 00:46:05.487 align:middle line:84%
because it's a bit more
convenient in this setting--

00:46:05.487 --> 00:46:11.330 align:middle line:90%


00:46:11.330 --> 00:46:16.720 align:middle line:90%
P of q equals 1 minus q.

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:19.560 align:middle line:84%
But notice here that this
inverse demand is actually

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:22.120 align:middle line:84%
exactly the same
demand we had before.

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:25.240 align:middle line:84%
Because if p of Q
equals 1 minus q, well,

00:46:25.240 --> 00:46:27.680 align:middle line:84%
how do I-- let's
solve for Q. That

00:46:27.680 --> 00:46:30.240 align:middle line:90%
means Q is equal to 1 minus p.

00:46:30.240 --> 00:46:32.560 align:middle line:84%
So this is exactly
the inverse demand

00:46:32.560 --> 00:46:35.880 align:middle line:84%
that is associated with
this demand over here.

00:46:35.880 --> 00:46:39.520 align:middle line:84%
So these are just two
different ways of writing it.

00:46:39.520 --> 00:46:44.080 align:middle line:84%
And again with the understanding
that if quantity gets too big,

00:46:44.080 --> 00:46:45.260 align:middle line:90%
then the price is just 0.

00:46:45.260 --> 00:46:46.760 align:middle line:84%
So if I want it to
be really formal,

00:46:46.760 --> 00:46:52.860 align:middle line:84%
I would say the maximum
of 1 minus q and 0.

00:46:52.860 --> 00:46:57.680 align:middle line:90%


00:46:57.680 --> 00:46:59.760 align:middle line:90%
But this is a crucial point.

00:46:59.760 --> 00:47:04.140 align:middle line:84%
This Q here is the
market quantity.

00:47:04.140 --> 00:47:10.080 align:middle line:90%


00:47:10.080 --> 00:47:13.960 align:middle line:84%
So the price at the
market depends on how much

00:47:13.960 --> 00:47:16.360 align:middle line:90%
water is brought to market.

00:47:16.360 --> 00:47:18.840 align:middle line:84%
But the amount of water or
apples brought to market

00:47:18.840 --> 00:47:22.600 align:middle line:84%
depends on the entire market,
not just an individual firm.

00:47:22.600 --> 00:47:28.760 align:middle line:84%
So the crucial thing here
is that, each firm i,

00:47:28.760 --> 00:47:32.910 align:middle line:90%
chooses a quantity qi.

00:47:32.910 --> 00:47:35.970 align:middle line:84%
So firm 1 chooses
their quantity, q1.

00:47:35.970 --> 00:47:38.490 align:middle line:84%
And firm 2 chooses
their quantity, q2.

00:47:38.490 --> 00:47:40.543 align:middle line:90%
And notice this is lowercase.

00:47:40.543 --> 00:47:43.210 align:middle line:84%
So we're generally going to use
lowercase to denote the quantity

00:47:43.210 --> 00:47:45.810 align:middle line:90%
chosen by a particular firm.

00:47:45.810 --> 00:47:50.610 align:middle line:84%
But the individual choices of
the firms, when added together,

00:47:50.610 --> 00:47:52.770 align:middle line:84%
determine what the
market demand is.

00:47:52.770 --> 00:47:56.530 align:middle line:84%
So in this case, the
market quantity Q

00:47:56.530 --> 00:47:59.930 align:middle line:84%
is going to be the sum of the
amount produced by each firm.

00:47:59.930 --> 00:48:04.510 align:middle line:84%
So this is going
to be q1 plus q2.

00:48:04.510 --> 00:48:13.170 align:middle line:90%


00:48:13.170 --> 00:48:15.970 align:middle line:84%
And the final assumption--
maybe I'll write it explicitly

00:48:15.970 --> 00:48:18.110 align:middle line:90%
is that firms maximize profits.

00:48:18.110 --> 00:48:20.930 align:middle line:90%


00:48:20.930 --> 00:48:22.730 align:middle line:84%
Expected profit--
or I guess there's

00:48:22.730 --> 00:48:26.130 align:middle line:90%
no [INAUDIBLE] say profit.

00:48:26.130 --> 00:48:28.730 align:middle line:84%
Now, remember, this is
an important assumption.

00:48:28.730 --> 00:48:31.050 align:middle line:84%
Going back to what we said
before, when we set up

00:48:31.050 --> 00:48:34.850 align:middle line:84%
game theory, we said what
people maximize is utility,

00:48:34.850 --> 00:48:37.450 align:middle line:84%
and utility is not
always the same as money.

00:48:37.450 --> 00:48:39.890 align:middle line:84%
So another way of saying
firms maximize profit

00:48:39.890 --> 00:48:42.930 align:middle line:90%
is that firms are risk neutral.

00:48:42.930 --> 00:48:45.250 align:middle line:84%
Their von
Neumann-Morgenstern utility

00:48:45.250 --> 00:48:48.450 align:middle line:84%
function as a function
of money is just linear.

00:48:48.450 --> 00:48:50.090 align:middle line:90%
Is this a reasonable assumption?

00:48:50.090 --> 00:48:51.030 align:middle line:90%
In some contexts, yes.

00:48:51.030 --> 00:48:52.225 align:middle line:90%
In other contexts, no.

00:48:52.225 --> 00:48:53.850 align:middle line:84%
So maybe in other
contexts, we'd really

00:48:53.850 --> 00:48:56.742 align:middle line:84%
want to think of a firm that's
risk averse over their profits.

00:48:56.742 --> 00:48:58.950 align:middle line:84%
But in this model, we're
assuming their risk neutral.

00:48:58.950 --> 00:49:02.610 align:middle line:84%
So all they're trying to
do is maximize profits.

00:49:02.610 --> 00:49:06.250 align:middle line:84%
OK, so now let's write
down what the profits are.

00:49:06.250 --> 00:49:08.410 align:middle line:90%
So let's look at firm 1.

00:49:08.410 --> 00:49:10.930 align:middle line:84%
It's easier to reason from
one firm's perspective.

00:49:10.930 --> 00:49:13.930 align:middle line:84%
Though, it's always
going to be symmetric.

00:49:13.930 --> 00:49:18.290 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to write
pi 1 of q1, q2.

00:49:18.290 --> 00:49:20.010 align:middle line:84%
So let's just
understand pi is often

00:49:20.010 --> 00:49:23.170 align:middle line:84%
used for profits just because
it's the Greek pi, like p

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:24.450 align:middle line:90%
for profit.

00:49:24.450 --> 00:49:26.070 align:middle line:84%
And we already
used p for prices,

00:49:26.070 --> 00:49:28.100 align:middle line:90%
so we use pi for profits.

00:49:28.100 --> 00:49:30.380 align:middle line:84%
What I want to
understand is I'm firm 1.

00:49:30.380 --> 00:49:33.780 align:middle line:84%
What is the profit
that I get if I

00:49:33.780 --> 00:49:36.300 align:middle line:84%
produce a quantity of
q1 and the other firm

00:49:36.300 --> 00:49:39.020 align:middle line:90%
produces a quantity of q2?

00:49:39.020 --> 00:49:40.660 align:middle line:90%
Well, let's understand this.

00:49:40.660 --> 00:49:43.680 align:middle line:84%
Well, just as before, we can
decompose this into two parts.

00:49:43.680 --> 00:49:47.240 align:middle line:84%
We have to keep track
of my profit per unit,

00:49:47.240 --> 00:49:49.402 align:middle line:84%
and then the number
of units I have.

00:49:49.402 --> 00:49:50.860 align:middle line:84%
The number of units
I have is easy.

00:49:50.860 --> 00:49:51.920 align:middle line:90%
Let's write that down.

00:49:51.920 --> 00:49:56.700 align:middle line:84%
That's q1-- q1, the
number of units I sell.

00:49:56.700 --> 00:50:01.100 align:middle line:84%
But then what is going
to be my profit per unit?

00:50:01.100 --> 00:50:02.860 align:middle line:90%
Thoughts here?

00:50:02.860 --> 00:50:06.680 align:middle line:84%
So what do I want
to multiply q1 by?

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:10.723 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: 1 minus q1 plus q2.

00:50:10.723 --> 00:50:12.640 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Yes, there's
a parentheses in there.

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:13.223 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, exactly.

00:50:13.223 --> 00:50:15.980 align:middle line:90%
So let me write it--

00:50:15.980 --> 00:50:18.300 align:middle line:90%
let me first write it as this.

00:50:18.300 --> 00:50:21.860 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll break it down
in the way you suggested.

00:50:21.860 --> 00:50:24.900 align:middle line:84%
So q1 is the amount
I'm producing.

00:50:24.900 --> 00:50:26.900 align:middle line:90%
What is my profit per unit?

00:50:26.900 --> 00:50:28.660 align:middle line:84%
Well, my profit
is just the price

00:50:28.660 --> 00:50:32.380 align:middle line:84%
that I'm able to sell it for
minus the cost of production.

00:50:32.380 --> 00:50:34.620 align:middle line:84%
But the tricky thing here
is, what is the price?

00:50:34.620 --> 00:50:37.700 align:middle line:84%
Well, the price is determined
by the market demand.

00:50:37.700 --> 00:50:41.580 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it's p of
the market quantity-- sorry,

00:50:41.580 --> 00:50:43.240 align:middle line:84%
not market demand,
market quantity--

00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:46.900 align:middle line:84%
which is the sum of q1
plus q2, the market supply.

00:50:46.900 --> 00:50:49.300 align:middle line:84%
And this is what makes
this a game theory problem.

00:50:49.300 --> 00:50:53.140 align:middle line:84%
Because how will I do as
firm 1 depends not only

00:50:53.140 --> 00:50:55.560 align:middle line:84%
on what I produce and
what I bring to market,

00:50:55.560 --> 00:50:58.620 align:middle line:84%
but also on what my opponent
brings to market, q2.

00:50:58.620 --> 00:51:01.780 align:middle line:84%
Why does it matter how
much my opponent produces?

00:51:01.780 --> 00:51:03.660 align:middle line:84%
Because how much my
opponent produces

00:51:03.660 --> 00:51:06.580 align:middle line:84%
affects the price at
which I sell my goods.

00:51:06.580 --> 00:51:08.500 align:middle line:84%
So now we have a
strategic interaction

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:11.180 align:middle line:90%
between the two firms.

00:51:11.180 --> 00:51:13.200 align:middle line:84%
And let's write
this out once more.

00:51:13.200 --> 00:51:19.820 align:middle line:84%
We have q1 times, well, p of
q1 plus q2 is just 1 minus--

00:51:19.820 --> 00:51:21.560 align:middle line:90%
in parentheses q1 plus q2.

00:51:21.560 --> 00:51:26.530 align:middle line:84%
So that's 1 minus
q1 minus q2 minus c.

00:51:26.530 --> 00:51:31.670 align:middle line:90%


00:51:31.670 --> 00:51:33.530 align:middle line:84%
So now it doesn't really
make sense to say,

00:51:33.530 --> 00:51:35.228 align:middle line:90%
what is my optimal quantity?

00:51:35.228 --> 00:51:37.770 align:middle line:84%
With the monopolist, we just
said what price should I charge?

00:51:37.770 --> 00:51:38.670 align:middle line:90%
That was easy.

00:51:38.670 --> 00:51:43.590 align:middle line:84%
Now the optimal quantity for
me depends upon the quantity

00:51:43.590 --> 00:51:45.910 align:middle line:90%
that the other firm produces.

00:51:45.910 --> 00:51:48.370 align:middle line:84%
So instead of just talking
about an optimal quantity,

00:51:48.370 --> 00:51:49.970 align:middle line:84%
we have to talk about
a best response.

00:51:49.970 --> 00:51:51.130 align:middle line:90%
Now we have a game.

00:51:51.130 --> 00:51:52.750 align:middle line:90%
Let's understand this as a game.

00:51:52.750 --> 00:51:55.510 align:middle line:84%
A strategy for firm
1 is a quantity.

00:51:55.510 --> 00:51:57.990 align:middle line:84%
A strategy for firm
2 is also a quantity.

00:51:57.990 --> 00:52:00.810 align:middle line:84%
And the utility of firm
1 is given like this.

00:52:00.810 --> 00:52:03.450 align:middle line:84%
And we can symmetrically write
down the utility for firm 2.

00:52:03.450 --> 00:52:06.550 align:middle line:84%
So now we have a game, and we
can talk about best responses.

00:52:06.550 --> 00:52:15.030 align:middle line:84%
So a best response by firm 1 to
a quantity q2 is equal to what?

00:52:15.030 --> 00:52:16.110 align:middle line:90%
Well?

00:52:16.110 --> 00:52:18.950 align:middle line:84%
We need to maximize
this expression.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:21.370 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of
expression comes up a lot.

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:22.970 align:middle line:84%
So I want to-- you
can do the algebra,

00:52:22.970 --> 00:52:24.845 align:middle line:84%
but I think it's nice
to know a little trick.

00:52:24.845 --> 00:52:28.230 align:middle line:84%
So if you're ever
maximizing something

00:52:28.230 --> 00:52:30.130 align:middle line:90%
like this where a is positive--

00:52:30.130 --> 00:52:32.870 align:middle line:90%


00:52:32.870 --> 00:52:37.590 align:middle line:84%
so I'm choosing x, to
maximize x times a minus x,

00:52:37.590 --> 00:52:39.370 align:middle line:84%
you can just derive
this with calculus.

00:52:39.370 --> 00:52:41.990 align:middle line:84%
But what you'll find
is x equals a over 2.

00:52:41.990 --> 00:52:43.430 align:middle line:90%
That's always the optimum.

00:52:43.430 --> 00:52:45.797 align:middle line:84%
And this kind of form
comes up again and again.

00:52:45.797 --> 00:52:47.630 align:middle line:84%
So it's just a nice
thing to know right away

00:52:47.630 --> 00:52:49.510 align:middle line:90%
for exams and quizzes.

00:52:49.510 --> 00:52:53.190 align:middle line:84%
Again, it's the fact
that a rectangle

00:52:53.190 --> 00:52:56.890 align:middle line:84%
of constant perimeter, its
area is maximized by a square.

00:52:56.890 --> 00:52:59.693 align:middle line:84%
If I take x equals a over
2, then both of these terms

00:52:59.693 --> 00:53:00.610 align:middle line:90%
are equal to a over 2.

00:53:00.610 --> 00:53:01.693 align:middle line:90%
That's the geometry of it.

00:53:01.693 --> 00:53:03.410 align:middle line:90%
You can do calculus to solve it.

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:05.810 align:middle line:84%
But now we can immediately
find what my best response is.

00:53:05.810 --> 00:53:08.890 align:middle line:84%
We just have to be careful
to figure out what the a is.

00:53:08.890 --> 00:53:11.470 align:middle line:84%
So we have to think,
OK, I'm firm 1.

00:53:11.470 --> 00:53:13.030 align:middle line:90%
I'm choosing q1.

00:53:13.030 --> 00:53:15.990 align:middle line:84%
So from my perspective, what
part of this do I control?

00:53:15.990 --> 00:53:19.430 align:middle line:84%
And what part of
this is a constant?

00:53:19.430 --> 00:53:20.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:53:20.240 --> 00:53:22.878 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: q1 is
controlled [INAUDIBLE].

00:53:22.878 --> 00:53:24.420 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, so
q1 is controlled.

00:53:24.420 --> 00:53:29.420 align:middle line:84%
So the a here is 1
minus q2 minus c.

00:53:29.420 --> 00:53:33.320 align:middle line:84%
We can think of this as
the constant a minus q1

00:53:33.320 --> 00:53:36.000 align:middle line:90%
where a is 1 minus q2 minus c.

00:53:36.000 --> 00:53:39.520 align:middle line:84%
So if I just do this out, I see
that my best response is 1 minus

00:53:39.520 --> 00:53:42.540 align:middle line:90%
q2 minus c divided by 2.

00:53:42.540 --> 00:53:51.520 align:middle line:90%


00:53:51.520 --> 00:53:54.400 align:middle line:84%
OK, and again, let's
ask if this makes sense.

00:53:54.400 --> 00:53:58.000 align:middle line:84%
So as before, we see
that the amount I produce

00:53:58.000 --> 00:54:00.000 align:middle line:90%
is decreasing in the cost.

00:54:00.000 --> 00:54:02.785 align:middle line:84%
So if the cost of production
is higher, I produce less.

00:54:02.785 --> 00:54:03.660 align:middle line:90%
Does this make sense?

00:54:03.660 --> 00:54:05.700 align:middle line:84%
Can someone give
intuition for this?

00:54:05.700 --> 00:54:08.360 align:middle line:84%
Why do I want to produce less
when the cost of production

00:54:08.360 --> 00:54:09.020 align:middle line:90%
is higher?

00:54:09.020 --> 00:54:12.198 align:middle line:90%


00:54:12.198 --> 00:54:13.740 align:middle line:84%
Why don't I just
keep producing more?

00:54:13.740 --> 00:54:17.760 align:middle line:90%


00:54:17.760 --> 00:54:18.300 align:middle line:90%
Thoughts?

00:54:18.300 --> 00:54:21.040 align:middle line:90%


00:54:21.040 --> 00:54:24.520 align:middle line:84%
Well, my amount of production
affects the market price.

00:54:24.520 --> 00:54:27.440 align:middle line:84%
So if the cost is really high,
it's only worth it for me

00:54:27.440 --> 00:54:29.560 align:middle line:84%
to produce if the
market price is high,

00:54:29.560 --> 00:54:31.380 align:middle line:84%
which means I can't
keep producing a lot.

00:54:31.380 --> 00:54:33.520 align:middle line:84%
Otherwise, I'd pull the
market price down too low.

00:54:33.520 --> 00:54:36.760 align:middle line:84%
So again, a higher cost
means I produce less simply

00:54:36.760 --> 00:54:41.040 align:middle line:84%
because I require a higher
price to make it worth it.

00:54:41.040 --> 00:54:44.000 align:middle line:84%
And then it's also
decreasing in q2.

00:54:44.000 --> 00:54:46.640 align:middle line:84%
So the more that my
competitor produces,

00:54:46.640 --> 00:54:48.400 align:middle line:90%
the less that I produce.

00:54:48.400 --> 00:54:49.420 align:middle line:90%
Does that make sense?

00:54:49.420 --> 00:54:56.440 align:middle line:90%


00:54:56.440 --> 00:54:58.240 align:middle line:90%
Well, if my-- yeah.

00:54:58.240 --> 00:54:58.920 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:54:58.920 --> 00:55:00.295 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They
are producing more

00:55:00.295 --> 00:55:01.580 align:middle line:90%
than the price is going down.

00:55:01.580 --> 00:55:02.580 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: Exactly right.

00:55:02.580 --> 00:55:05.400 align:middle line:84%
So the more they produce,
the lower the price

00:55:05.400 --> 00:55:07.940 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to face for a given
amount of my production.

00:55:07.940 --> 00:55:10.840 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, it's optimal
for me to produce less.

00:55:10.840 --> 00:55:13.980 align:middle line:84%
So now we've computed
the best responses.

00:55:13.980 --> 00:55:15.480 align:middle line:84%
I've only done it
one way, but we

00:55:15.480 --> 00:55:17.570 align:middle line:84%
can see what it's going
to be for the other firm.

00:55:17.570 --> 00:55:20.390 align:middle line:84%
BR2 of q1 is just
going to be symmetric.

00:55:20.390 --> 00:55:24.570 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be 1
minus q1 minus c over 2.

00:55:24.570 --> 00:55:28.850 align:middle line:84%
So if I'm firm 2 and firm 1
is choosing a quantity of q1,

00:55:28.850 --> 00:55:33.130 align:middle line:84%
this is the optimal quantity
for firm 2 to produce.

00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:36.770 align:middle line:84%
And now I want to solve
for an equilibrium, which

00:55:36.770 --> 00:55:40.530 align:middle line:90%
means I need--

00:55:40.530 --> 00:55:42.190 align:middle line:84%
maybe I'll just
write it like this.

00:55:42.190 --> 00:55:47.250 align:middle line:90%


00:55:47.250 --> 00:55:49.930 align:middle line:90%
Maybe I'll put these in here.

00:55:49.930 --> 00:55:51.910 align:middle line:84%
So what is my
equilibrium condition?

00:55:51.910 --> 00:55:55.370 align:middle line:84%
In equilibrium, each firm
is playing a best response

00:55:55.370 --> 00:55:57.610 align:middle line:90%
to the other firm's quantity.

00:55:57.610 --> 00:56:01.210 align:middle line:84%
So I need q1 star, what
firm 1 is choosing,

00:56:01.210 --> 00:56:05.330 align:middle line:84%
to be a best response to
what q2, firm 2, is choosing.

00:56:05.330 --> 00:56:08.770 align:middle line:84%
And I need the amount of
produced by firm 2, q2 star,

00:56:08.770 --> 00:56:11.490 align:middle line:84%
to be a best response for
firm 2 to the amount produced

00:56:11.490 --> 00:56:14.290 align:middle line:90%
by firm 1.

00:56:14.290 --> 00:56:17.450 align:middle line:84%
So I now have a
system of equations.

00:56:17.450 --> 00:56:19.610 align:middle line:84%
But let's understand,
my system of equations

00:56:19.610 --> 00:56:21.770 align:middle line:90%
is a linear system of equations.

00:56:21.770 --> 00:56:24.930 align:middle line:84%
And it has two equations
and two unknowns.

00:56:24.930 --> 00:56:27.770 align:middle line:84%
And in general, we can solve
those systems pretty easily.

00:56:27.770 --> 00:56:30.610 align:middle line:84%
We could go through the algebra,
but I'll spare you the algebra.

00:56:30.610 --> 00:56:37.730 align:middle line:84%
And what we'll get is q1
star equals q2 star equals

00:56:37.730 --> 00:56:39.830 align:middle line:90%
1 minus c over 3.

00:56:39.830 --> 00:56:51.370 align:middle line:90%


00:56:51.370 --> 00:56:53.130 align:middle line:84%
And let's ask,
what is the market

00:56:53.130 --> 00:56:55.770 align:middle line:90%
price going to be in this case?

00:56:55.770 --> 00:57:03.530 align:middle line:84%
Well, the market price is going
to be 1 minus q1 minus q2.

00:57:03.530 --> 00:57:06.570 align:middle line:84%
But each of these
is 1 minus c over 3,

00:57:06.570 --> 00:57:08.370 align:middle line:90%
but I subtract that twice.

00:57:08.370 --> 00:57:14.440 align:middle line:90%
So I get 1 minus 2/3 1 minus c.

00:57:14.440 --> 00:57:18.620 align:middle line:90%


00:57:18.620 --> 00:57:21.460 align:middle line:84%
And again, it's
nice to pull c out.

00:57:21.460 --> 00:57:23.040 align:middle line:90%
So we can do a little trick.

00:57:23.040 --> 00:57:24.100 align:middle line:90%
This is c.

00:57:24.100 --> 00:57:26.140 align:middle line:90%
Let's add c and subtract c.

00:57:26.140 --> 00:57:31.200 align:middle line:84%
And what we get is c
plus 1 minus c over 3.

00:57:31.200 --> 00:57:42.740 align:middle line:90%


00:57:42.740 --> 00:57:45.140 align:middle line:84%
So one thing we can
make a comparison

00:57:45.140 --> 00:57:46.340 align:middle line:90%
with our monopoly case.

00:57:46.340 --> 00:57:49.020 align:middle line:84%
In our monopoly case where
we had only one firm,

00:57:49.020 --> 00:57:52.420 align:middle line:84%
the markup was 1
minus c1 over 2.

00:57:52.420 --> 00:57:55.020 align:middle line:84%
Here, when we have two
firms competing in Cournot,

00:57:55.020 --> 00:57:57.420 align:middle line:90%
the markup is 1 minus c over 3.

00:57:57.420 --> 00:58:03.260 align:middle line:84%
So we see that we have a smaller
markup, which maybe makes sense.

00:58:03.260 --> 00:58:05.540 align:middle line:84%
And we can actually
generalize this.

00:58:05.540 --> 00:58:09.120 align:middle line:84%
So let's understand
it intuitively.

00:58:09.120 --> 00:58:22.980 align:middle line:90%


00:58:22.980 --> 00:58:31.560 align:middle line:84%
So let's look at the
general case with n firms.

00:58:31.560 --> 00:58:34.022 align:middle line:90%


00:58:34.022 --> 00:58:35.980 align:middle line:84%
And this we can actually
just guess by the form

00:58:35.980 --> 00:58:36.730 align:middle line:90%
that we have here.

00:58:36.730 --> 00:58:39.120 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to go
through the derivation,

00:58:39.120 --> 00:58:42.100 align:middle line:84%
but I'm just going to
write down the answer.

00:58:42.100 --> 00:58:44.660 align:middle line:84%
And it's pretty easy to check
using the same argument.

00:58:44.660 --> 00:58:46.580 align:middle line:90%
So we have n firms.

00:58:46.580 --> 00:58:50.260 align:middle line:84%
How much is each firm
going to produce?

00:58:50.260 --> 00:58:54.220 align:middle line:84%
Maybe I'll write q1
star equals qn star.

00:58:54.220 --> 00:58:56.140 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out
with n firms, we're

00:58:56.140 --> 00:58:57.880 align:middle line:84%
going to have a
symmetric equilibrium,

00:58:57.880 --> 00:59:00.620 align:middle line:84%
every firm is going to
produce the same amount,

00:59:00.620 --> 00:59:03.200 align:middle line:84%
and based on our
pattern, with two firms,

00:59:03.200 --> 00:59:05.980 align:middle line:84%
they each produce
1 minus c over 3.

00:59:05.980 --> 00:59:10.230 align:middle line:84%
And notice in the
monopoly case, we got--

00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:11.430 align:middle line:90%
did we solve for q?

00:59:11.430 --> 00:59:14.082 align:middle line:90%
1 minus c over 2.

00:59:14.082 --> 00:59:16.290 align:middle line:84%
Now, in the monopoly case,
they were choosing prices.

00:59:16.290 --> 00:59:17.870 align:middle line:84%
But actually, with a
monopolist, whether you choose

00:59:17.870 --> 00:59:20.190 align:middle line:84%
price or quantity doesn't
make a difference.

00:59:20.190 --> 00:59:22.390 align:middle line:84%
So with one firm, we
got 1 minus c over 2.

00:59:22.390 --> 00:59:25.593 align:middle line:84%
With two firms, we
got 1 minus c over 3.

00:59:25.593 --> 00:59:27.010 align:middle line:84%
It's a pretty clear
pattern there.

00:59:27.010 --> 00:59:31.070 align:middle line:84%
It's always 1 minus c divided
by the number of firms plus 1.

00:59:31.070 --> 00:59:36.843 align:middle line:84%
So this is 1 minus
c over n plus 1.

00:59:36.843 --> 00:59:38.010 align:middle line:90%
So I think this makes sense.

00:59:38.010 --> 00:59:40.670 align:middle line:84%
Let's again go
through our intuition.

00:59:40.670 --> 00:59:43.250 align:middle line:84%
Each firm produces less
when the cost is higher.

00:59:43.250 --> 00:59:44.430 align:middle line:90%
That makes sense.

00:59:44.430 --> 00:59:47.670 align:middle line:84%
Each firm also produces less
when the number of other firms

00:59:47.670 --> 00:59:48.630 align:middle line:90%
goes up.

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:53.070 align:middle line:84%
So the more firms there are,
the less each firm produces.

00:59:53.070 --> 00:59:55.910 align:middle line:84%
And the reason is that
the residual demand

00:59:55.910 --> 00:59:57.610 align:middle line:90%
that they can capture is lower.

00:59:57.610 --> 01:00:00.652 align:middle line:84%
The other firms are already
capturing a lot of the market.

01:00:00.652 --> 01:00:03.110 align:middle line:84%
Or another way of saying it
is, the other firms are already

01:00:03.110 --> 01:00:04.950 align:middle line:90%
dragging down the price.

01:00:04.950 --> 01:00:08.230 align:middle line:84%
So I don't want to
bring so much more.

01:00:08.230 --> 01:00:13.950 align:middle line:84%
It's also good, though, to look
at the total market quantity.

01:00:13.950 --> 01:00:22.310 align:middle line:84%
So Q star which equals q1
star up to qn star is--

01:00:22.310 --> 01:00:24.770 align:middle line:84%
let's sum up the amount
produced by each firm.

01:00:24.770 --> 01:00:27.950 align:middle line:84%
So we can look at the total
amount produced by the market.

01:00:27.950 --> 01:00:35.350 align:middle line:84%
And what we're going to get is
n over n plus 1 times 1 over c.

01:00:35.350 --> 01:00:40.230 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is that
this is decreasing in n,

01:00:40.230 --> 01:00:42.710 align:middle line:90%
but this is increasing in n.

01:00:42.710 --> 01:00:46.350 align:middle line:84%
So when there's more firms,
each firm produces less.

01:00:46.350 --> 01:00:48.990 align:middle line:84%
But the total amount
produced by all firms

01:00:48.990 --> 01:00:50.870 align:middle line:90%
is actually increasing.

01:00:50.870 --> 01:00:56.110 align:middle line:84%
And as n gets really large,
what is this going to approach?

01:00:56.110 --> 01:00:58.510 align:middle line:84%
Just 1 minus c because
n over n plus 1

01:00:58.510 --> 01:01:00.470 align:middle line:90%
is going to converge to 1.

01:01:00.470 --> 01:01:02.310 align:middle line:90%
So we just get 1 minus c.

01:01:02.310 --> 01:01:05.880 align:middle line:84%
So I'll say, and this
converges to 1 minus c as n

01:01:05.880 --> 01:01:06.780 align:middle line:90%
goes to infinity.

01:01:06.780 --> 01:01:11.360 align:middle line:90%


01:01:11.360 --> 01:01:15.400 align:middle line:90%
And what about the market price?

01:01:15.400 --> 01:01:18.400 align:middle line:84%
Remember, it doesn't make sense
to talk about the firm's price

01:01:18.400 --> 01:01:20.700 align:middle line:84%
because they're all
selling at the same price.

01:01:20.700 --> 01:01:24.380 align:middle line:84%
So there's a firm quantity
and a market quantity,

01:01:24.380 --> 01:01:25.820 align:middle line:90%
but there's only a market price.

01:01:25.820 --> 01:01:29.880 align:middle line:84%
There's no individual
firm price in this model.

01:01:29.880 --> 01:01:32.420 align:middle line:90%
Again, we can see the pattern.

01:01:32.420 --> 01:01:36.220 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be c plus
1 minus c over n plus 1.

01:01:36.220 --> 01:01:39.600 align:middle line:90%


01:01:39.600 --> 01:01:41.740 align:middle line:90%
And this is decreasing in it.

01:01:41.740 --> 01:01:45.000 align:middle line:90%


01:01:45.000 --> 01:01:46.620 align:middle line:84%
And again, I think
this makes sense.

01:01:46.620 --> 01:01:50.160 align:middle line:84%
The more firms there
are, the stiffer

01:01:50.160 --> 01:01:52.640 align:middle line:84%
the competition
between the firms,

01:01:52.640 --> 01:01:56.080 align:middle line:90%
and the lower the price is.

01:01:56.080 --> 01:02:03.040 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, what happens to
the markup as n gets really big?

01:02:03.040 --> 01:02:05.025 align:middle line:90%
The markup goes to 0.

01:02:05.025 --> 01:02:06.400 align:middle line:84%
So what we see is
that the market

01:02:06.400 --> 01:02:13.000 align:middle line:84%
price in this case converges
to c as n goes to infinity.

01:02:13.000 --> 01:02:15.880 align:middle line:84%
And this exactly captures
perfect competition.

01:02:15.880 --> 01:02:18.880 align:middle line:84%
So when there are many, many
firms competing with each other,

01:02:18.880 --> 01:02:22.680 align:middle line:84%
they no longer have the market
power to charge a markup.

01:02:22.680 --> 01:02:25.000 align:middle line:84%
And in the limit, as we
have many, many firms,

01:02:25.000 --> 01:02:27.400 align:middle line:90%
every single firm--

01:02:27.400 --> 01:02:29.920 align:middle line:84%
well, every firm is going
to produce a quantity such

01:02:29.920 --> 01:02:34.480 align:middle line:84%
that the market price is
exactly equal to cost of c.

01:02:34.480 --> 01:02:41.480 align:middle line:84%
And can anyone
interpret 1 minus c?

01:02:41.480 --> 01:02:44.700 align:middle line:84%
Why do we get 1 minus c as
the quantity in this case?

01:02:44.700 --> 01:02:48.863 align:middle line:90%


01:02:48.863 --> 01:02:50.280 align:middle line:84%
It turns out this
is what we might

01:02:50.280 --> 01:02:51.820 align:middle line:90%
call the efficient quantity.

01:02:51.820 --> 01:02:58.053 align:middle line:90%


01:02:58.053 --> 01:02:59.220 align:middle line:90%
What do I mean by efficient?

01:02:59.220 --> 01:03:02.450 align:middle line:84%
Well, if it costs c
to produce a good,

01:03:02.450 --> 01:03:04.590 align:middle line:84%
for whom is it efficient
to buy the good?

01:03:04.590 --> 01:03:07.570 align:middle line:84%
It's efficient for people to
buy the good if they value it

01:03:07.570 --> 01:03:09.930 align:middle line:84%
more than the cost
of production.

01:03:09.930 --> 01:03:13.450 align:middle line:84%
But our demand curve
exactly tells us

01:03:13.450 --> 01:03:16.050 align:middle line:84%
that the number of
people who value

01:03:16.050 --> 01:03:18.690 align:middle line:84%
the product, or more
precisely, the number of units

01:03:18.690 --> 01:03:21.650 align:middle line:84%
of the product that have
marginal utility above c

01:03:21.650 --> 01:03:23.173 align:middle line:90%
is exactly 1 minus c.

01:03:23.173 --> 01:03:24.590 align:middle line:84%
So what happens
when we have many,

01:03:24.590 --> 01:03:27.450 align:middle line:84%
many firms is we converge
to an efficient outcome.

01:03:27.450 --> 01:03:30.527 align:middle line:84%
The market price is
exactly c, and the amount

01:03:30.527 --> 01:03:32.610 align:middle line:84%
that's produced and consumed
is exactly the amount

01:03:32.610 --> 01:03:35.810 align:middle line:84%
that it's efficient to
produce and consume.

01:03:35.810 --> 01:03:39.950 align:middle line:84%
People who value each unit at
more than c are going to buy it,

01:03:39.950 --> 01:03:43.370 align:middle line:84%
and people who don't value it
at more than c don't buy it.

01:03:43.370 --> 01:03:47.730 align:middle line:84%
So we see this is an
initial illustration

01:03:47.730 --> 01:03:55.190 align:middle line:84%
that perfect competition
leads us to efficiency.

01:03:55.190 --> 01:03:57.850 align:middle line:90%


01:03:57.850 --> 01:04:00.170 align:middle line:84%
And here, perfect
competition meaning the limit

01:04:00.170 --> 01:04:02.870 align:middle line:84%
as the number of identical
firms grows very, very large.

01:04:02.870 --> 01:04:12.090 align:middle line:90%


01:04:12.090 --> 01:04:14.030 align:middle line:90%
Great, any questions about this?

01:04:14.030 --> 01:04:17.850 align:middle line:90%


01:04:17.850 --> 01:04:18.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:04:18.370 --> 01:04:24.650 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Compared to some other
quantity other than 1 minus c,

01:04:24.650 --> 01:04:26.985 align:middle line:84%
why are those other
quantities inefficient?

01:04:26.985 --> 01:04:28.610 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Great, so
let's look at the--

01:04:28.610 --> 01:04:35.113 align:middle line:84%
do we have a demand
curve somewhere?

01:04:35.113 --> 01:04:36.530 align:middle line:84%
I forget if I've
erased something.

01:04:36.530 --> 01:04:38.970 align:middle line:90%
I may have erased it.

01:04:38.970 --> 01:04:41.530 align:middle line:84%
So let me just draw
a little curve here.

01:04:41.530 --> 01:04:45.890 align:middle line:84%
So one way to interpret, this
comes down to how we interpret

01:04:45.890 --> 01:04:56.355 align:middle line:84%
demand Q of P. So what
Q of P means, let's

01:04:56.355 --> 01:04:57.730 align:middle line:84%
imagine just to
make the argument

01:04:57.730 --> 01:05:00.800 align:middle line:84%
simpler that everyone only
consumes one unit of the good.

01:05:00.800 --> 01:05:02.720 align:middle line:84%
So when demand's going
up, as price drops,

01:05:02.720 --> 01:05:04.752 align:middle line:84%
it's because more people
are choosing to buy,

01:05:04.752 --> 01:05:06.960 align:middle line:84%
not because a single person
is buying multiple units.

01:05:06.960 --> 01:05:09.460 align:middle line:84%
The argument goes through, but
that'll just make it simpler.

01:05:09.460 --> 01:05:12.940 align:middle line:84%
So what Q of P is,
it's the number

01:05:12.940 --> 01:05:16.020 align:middle line:84%
of people who are willing
to buy at price p.

01:05:16.020 --> 01:05:23.090 align:middle line:90%


01:05:23.090 --> 01:05:25.340 align:middle line:84%
But let's think this through
a little bit differently.

01:05:25.340 --> 01:05:27.540 align:middle line:90%
Who's willing to buy at price p?

01:05:27.540 --> 01:05:30.980 align:middle line:84%
It's people whose value
for the good is at least p.

01:05:30.980 --> 01:05:33.700 align:middle line:84%
So another way of thinking of
demand Q of P is-- actually,

01:05:33.700 --> 01:05:35.460 align:middle line:90%
it's the number of people--

01:05:35.460 --> 01:05:40.760 align:middle line:84%
it's the number with
value at least p.

01:05:40.760 --> 01:05:43.620 align:middle line:90%


01:05:43.620 --> 01:05:46.500 align:middle line:84%
It's the number of people who
say, for them, the apple is

01:05:46.500 --> 01:05:48.500 align:middle line:90%
worth at least p.

01:05:48.500 --> 01:05:52.940 align:middle line:84%
But that means that Q of
c, which in our example

01:05:52.940 --> 01:05:56.220 align:middle line:84%
is exactly 1 minus c,
it's the demand at c,

01:05:56.220 --> 01:06:00.960 align:middle line:84%
is exactly the number of people
whose value is at least cost.

01:06:00.960 --> 01:06:07.940 align:middle line:90%


01:06:07.940 --> 01:06:11.000 align:middle line:84%
So this captures-- if we look
at everyone in the population,

01:06:11.000 --> 01:06:13.820 align:middle line:84%
some people value the good at
more than it costs to produce.

01:06:13.820 --> 01:06:16.280 align:middle line:84%
Some people valued at less
than it cost to produce.

01:06:16.280 --> 01:06:19.020 align:middle line:84%
The efficient
allocation is for people

01:06:19.020 --> 01:06:21.940 align:middle line:84%
to get the good exactly if
their value for the good

01:06:21.940 --> 01:06:24.180 align:middle line:84%
exceeds the cost
of producing it.

01:06:24.180 --> 01:06:26.577 align:middle line:84%
And in this case, that
turns out to be 1 minus c.

01:06:26.577 --> 01:06:28.660 align:middle line:84%
It's the number of people
whose value for the good

01:06:28.660 --> 01:06:31.100 align:middle line:90%
is more than 1 minus c.

01:06:31.100 --> 01:06:32.040 align:middle line:90%
Any other questions?

01:06:32.040 --> 01:06:34.860 align:middle line:90%


01:06:34.860 --> 01:06:36.700 align:middle line:84%
Great, OK, so now
that we've done

01:06:36.700 --> 01:06:39.140 align:middle line:84%
Cournot in the last
few minutes, let's

01:06:39.140 --> 01:06:41.900 align:middle line:90%
move to Bertrand competition.

01:06:41.900 --> 01:06:46.860 align:middle line:90%
So maybe come down.

01:06:46.860 --> 01:06:50.307 align:middle line:84%
No, I guess I'm going to
get stuck with these boards.

01:06:50.307 --> 01:06:51.640 align:middle line:90%
I'll come all the way over here.

01:06:51.640 --> 01:07:11.150 align:middle line:90%


01:07:11.150 --> 01:07:15.870 align:middle line:84%
So now let's look at
Bertrand competition.

01:07:15.870 --> 01:07:17.970 align:middle line:84%
So almost everything's
the same as before.

01:07:17.970 --> 01:07:19.390 align:middle line:90%
We still have two firms.

01:07:19.390 --> 01:07:21.550 align:middle line:84%
They still have constant
marginal cost of c,

01:07:21.550 --> 01:07:24.310 align:middle line:84%
and demand still takes the
same firm-- the same form.

01:07:24.310 --> 01:07:28.890 align:middle line:84%
So firms, I'll just write
it quickly, firms 1 and 2.

01:07:28.890 --> 01:07:32.470 align:middle line:90%


01:07:32.470 --> 01:07:34.530 align:middle line:90%
Constant marginal cost c.

01:07:34.530 --> 01:07:38.110 align:middle line:90%


01:07:38.110 --> 01:07:40.010 align:middle line:90%
And the market demand.

01:07:40.010 --> 01:07:45.358 align:middle line:90%


01:07:45.358 --> 01:07:47.150 align:middle line:84%
And this time it's more
convenient to write

01:07:47.150 --> 01:07:51.510 align:middle line:84%
it this way, the demand Q
of P equals the max of 1

01:07:51.510 --> 01:07:55.030 align:middle line:90%
minus p and 0.

01:07:55.030 --> 01:08:01.230 align:middle line:84%
And c where c is
between 0 and 1.

01:08:01.230 --> 01:08:02.870 align:middle line:84%
But now the difference
with Bertrand

01:08:02.870 --> 01:08:05.770 align:middle line:84%
is that we imagine that, instead
of firms choosing quantities,

01:08:05.770 --> 01:08:08.790 align:middle line:90%
they choose prices.

01:08:08.790 --> 01:08:20.417 align:middle line:84%
So each firm i
chooses a price pi.

01:08:20.417 --> 01:08:22.750 align:middle line:84%
And now we have to try to
understand, what are consumers

01:08:22.750 --> 01:08:23.750 align:middle line:90%
going to do?

01:08:23.750 --> 01:08:26.950 align:middle line:84%
If firm 1 charges a
price of p1 and firm 2

01:08:26.950 --> 01:08:31.750 align:middle line:84%
chooses a price of p2, what
would you do as a consumer?

01:08:31.750 --> 01:08:34.950 align:middle line:90%
Which firm would you buy from?

01:08:34.950 --> 01:08:36.210 align:middle line:90%
The firm with the lower price.

01:08:36.210 --> 01:08:41.630 align:middle line:84%
So our assumption is going to be
that, whenever the firms charge

01:08:41.630 --> 01:08:46.910 align:middle line:84%
different prices, all consumers
buy from the lower-priced firm.

01:08:46.910 --> 01:08:48.670 align:middle line:90%
And how many units do they buy?

01:08:48.670 --> 01:08:53.080 align:middle line:84%
Well, they buy from the market
demand at that lower price.

01:08:53.080 --> 01:09:01.420 align:middle line:84%
So let's see if we can
write down a formula.

01:09:01.420 --> 01:09:07.760 align:middle line:90%


01:09:07.760 --> 01:09:10.660 align:middle line:84%
So mathematically, so we
understand, this is a story.

01:09:10.660 --> 01:09:12.100 align:middle line:90%
This is the motivating story.

01:09:12.100 --> 01:09:14.040 align:middle line:84%
But mathematically,
this is just a game.

01:09:14.040 --> 01:09:16.760 align:middle line:84%
And we have to specify the
strategies and the payoffs.

01:09:16.760 --> 01:09:18.520 align:middle line:84%
We've already specified
the strategies

01:09:18.520 --> 01:09:21.279 align:middle line:84%
because what each firm
chooses is a price.

01:09:21.279 --> 01:09:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So to finish specifying this
mathematically as a game,

01:09:24.000 --> 01:09:26.840 align:middle line:84%
we need to say, what
is each firm's payoff

01:09:26.840 --> 01:09:30.000 align:middle line:84%
as a function of the
price she charges

01:09:30.000 --> 01:09:32.880 align:middle line:84%
and the price that the
other firm charges?

01:09:32.880 --> 01:09:33.700 align:middle line:90%
So let's do it.

01:09:33.700 --> 01:09:37.560 align:middle line:84%
It's symmetric, so let's do it
from the perspective of firm 1.

01:09:37.560 --> 01:09:42.120 align:middle line:84%
What I want to understand
is pi 1 of p1, p2.

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:45.300 align:middle line:84%
So as before, we're going
to use pi to denote profit.

01:09:45.300 --> 01:09:48.920 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to write pi 1 to
say this is firm 1's profit.

01:09:48.920 --> 01:09:51.800 align:middle line:84%
But now from 1's profit is not
a function of the quantities

01:09:51.800 --> 01:09:57.000 align:middle line:84%
we choose, but it's a function
of the prices that we choose.

01:09:57.000 --> 01:10:00.400 align:middle line:84%
And this is going to take
a different form depending

01:10:00.400 --> 01:10:02.500 align:middle line:84%
on the relative
values of p1 and p2.

01:10:02.500 --> 01:10:04.320 align:middle line:84%
So let me break
this up into cases.

01:10:04.320 --> 01:10:07.240 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be the case
where p1 is less than p2.

01:10:07.240 --> 01:10:10.320 align:middle line:84%
There's going to be the one
where-- case where p1 equals p2

01:10:10.320 --> 01:10:12.500 align:middle line:84%
and the case where p1
is greater than p2.

01:10:12.500 --> 01:10:14.933 align:middle line:90%


01:10:14.933 --> 01:10:16.600 align:middle line:84%
All right, so let's
try to fill this in.

01:10:16.600 --> 01:10:18.183 align:middle line:84%
I think this is the
easiest case here.

01:10:18.183 --> 01:10:20.280 align:middle line:84%
So if I charge more
than my opponent,

01:10:20.280 --> 01:10:23.200 align:middle line:90%
what is my profit going to be?

01:10:23.200 --> 01:10:23.860 align:middle line:90%
Just 0, right?

01:10:23.860 --> 01:10:27.400 align:middle line:90%
I'm not going to sell anything.

01:10:27.400 --> 01:10:29.880 align:middle line:90%
So I get 0 here.

01:10:29.880 --> 01:10:31.180 align:middle line:90%
Now let's do this case.

01:10:31.180 --> 01:10:34.400 align:middle line:84%
If I charge less than
my opponent, well,

01:10:34.400 --> 01:10:37.593 align:middle line:84%
now let's first-- let's
break it up into two pieces

01:10:37.593 --> 01:10:38.260 align:middle line:90%
as we always do.

01:10:38.260 --> 01:10:41.400 align:middle line:84%
We need to think about,
what is my profit per unit?

01:10:41.400 --> 01:10:44.480 align:middle line:84%
And then what is the number
of units I'm going to sell?

01:10:44.480 --> 01:10:49.230 align:middle line:84%
So my profit per unit is
going to be p1 minus c.

01:10:49.230 --> 01:10:54.570 align:middle line:84%
I'm selling at a price
of p1, and my cost is c.

01:10:54.570 --> 01:10:57.210 align:middle line:84%
And notice how this is
dual to what we did before.

01:10:57.210 --> 01:10:59.890 align:middle line:84%
Before, I directly
chose quantity,

01:10:59.890 --> 01:11:02.730 align:middle line:84%
but the price depended on
what the other person did.

01:11:02.730 --> 01:11:05.290 align:middle line:84%
Here, I directly choose
price, but the quantity

01:11:05.290 --> 01:11:08.370 align:middle line:84%
depends on what the other
person does and also what I do.

01:11:08.370 --> 01:11:12.510 align:middle line:90%
So what is going to go in here?

01:11:12.510 --> 01:11:17.650 align:middle line:84%
What is going to be the
quantity I sell at price p1?

01:11:17.650 --> 01:11:20.553 align:middle line:84%
Well, everyone, if they buy,
is going to buy from me.

01:11:20.553 --> 01:11:21.970 align:middle line:84%
So the amount that
I sell is going

01:11:21.970 --> 01:11:25.130 align:middle line:84%
to be the market demand
at a price of p1.

01:11:25.130 --> 01:11:29.090 align:middle line:84%
But we already said the market
demand at a price of p is just 1

01:11:29.090 --> 01:11:30.130 align:middle line:90%
minus p.

01:11:30.130 --> 01:11:33.690 align:middle line:84%
So we're going to
get 1 minus p1,

01:11:33.690 --> 01:11:36.198 align:middle line:84%
again, assuming
p1 is less than 1,

01:11:36.198 --> 01:11:37.990 align:middle line:84%
but we're going to be
a little sloppy here.

01:11:37.990 --> 01:11:42.090 align:middle line:90%


01:11:42.090 --> 01:11:45.850 align:middle line:84%
And now we see the same central
trade off but in the other way.

01:11:45.850 --> 01:11:48.670 align:middle line:84%
If I raise my price,
I get more per unit,

01:11:48.670 --> 01:11:50.890 align:middle line:84%
but the number of
units I sell goes down.

01:11:50.890 --> 01:11:51.830 align:middle line:90%
What about this case?

01:11:51.830 --> 01:11:52.430 align:middle line:90%
This is a little tricky.

01:11:52.430 --> 01:11:53.680 align:middle line:90%
We have to make an assumption.

01:11:53.680 --> 01:11:57.530 align:middle line:84%
What happens if both firms
charge exactly the same price?

01:11:57.530 --> 01:11:58.050 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:11:58.050 --> 01:11:59.490 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You both get
half of the market.

01:11:59.490 --> 01:12:00.530 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Right, that's
the standard assumption

01:12:00.530 --> 01:12:02.250 align:middle line:84%
that if consumers are
indifferent, maybe

01:12:02.250 --> 01:12:05.470 align:middle line:84%
they're randomize, maybe they,
whatever, for whatever reason,

01:12:05.470 --> 01:12:06.650 align:middle line:90%
it's going to be half/half.

01:12:06.650 --> 01:12:12.530 align:middle line:84%
So it's going to be p1 minus
c times 1 minus p1 over 2.

01:12:12.530 --> 01:12:15.250 align:middle line:84%
So we're both charging
the same price p1.

01:12:15.250 --> 01:12:18.650 align:middle line:84%
The market demand is 1 minus
p1, but I get half of that,

01:12:18.650 --> 01:12:21.330 align:middle line:84%
and my opponent
gets half of that.

01:12:21.330 --> 01:12:23.470 align:middle line:84%
So now, mathematically,
we're done.

01:12:23.470 --> 01:12:26.330 align:middle line:90%
We've described the game.

01:12:26.330 --> 01:12:29.570 align:middle line:84%
Now what we want to understand
is the best response.

01:12:29.570 --> 01:12:34.490 align:middle line:84%
So now let me understand
the best response of firm 1

01:12:34.490 --> 01:12:37.730 align:middle line:90%
to a price of p2.

01:12:37.730 --> 01:12:39.450 align:middle line:84%
And there's going
to be a few cases

01:12:39.450 --> 01:12:41.090 align:middle line:90%
we have to be careful about.

01:12:41.090 --> 01:12:43.250 align:middle line:90%
So let's break this down.

01:12:43.250 --> 01:12:46.300 align:middle line:84%
The first case is if
p2 is less than c.

01:12:46.300 --> 01:12:49.500 align:middle line:90%
Next case is p2 equals c.

01:12:49.500 --> 01:12:52.040 align:middle line:90%
Next case is c is less than p2.

01:12:52.040 --> 01:12:54.860 align:middle line:90%


01:12:54.860 --> 01:12:56.380 align:middle line:84%
And this is going
to be a final case

01:12:56.380 --> 01:12:57.740 align:middle line:90%
at the end, which I'll get to.

01:12:57.740 --> 01:13:00.620 align:middle line:84%
But let's go through
it step by step.

01:13:00.620 --> 01:13:05.140 align:middle line:84%
What if my opponent charges
a price less than c?

01:13:05.140 --> 01:13:07.420 align:middle line:90%
Strictly less than c.

01:13:07.420 --> 01:13:08.880 align:middle line:84%
What price would
I like to charge?

01:13:08.880 --> 01:13:09.380 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:13:09.380 --> 01:13:10.797 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well,
at that point, you

01:13:10.797 --> 01:13:14.020 align:middle line:84%
should just not because
if you compete with them

01:13:14.020 --> 01:13:15.860 align:middle line:84%
and actually get
customers, you're not

01:13:15.860 --> 01:13:17.280 align:middle line:90%
going to be making any money.

01:13:17.280 --> 01:13:19.020 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Great,
so the only way I'm

01:13:19.020 --> 01:13:20.660 align:middle line:84%
going to be able
to sell to anyone

01:13:20.660 --> 01:13:24.780 align:middle line:84%
is if I charge a price
that's at most p2,

01:13:24.780 --> 01:13:27.140 align:middle line:84%
but that price is already
below c, so I'm definitely

01:13:27.140 --> 01:13:28.200 align:middle line:90%
going to lose money.

01:13:28.200 --> 01:13:30.380 align:middle line:84%
So the best I can do
is get a payoff of 0.

01:13:30.380 --> 01:13:34.940 align:middle line:84%
But what price will ensure
that I get a payoff of 0?

01:13:34.940 --> 01:13:38.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, 0 actually would not
be a very good price to set.

01:13:38.180 --> 01:13:39.120 align:middle line:90%
I need to charge.

01:13:39.120 --> 01:13:42.540 align:middle line:84%
Well, let's see, there's a
lot of things I could do.

01:13:42.540 --> 01:13:46.940 align:middle line:90%
I can actually charge.

01:13:46.940 --> 01:13:50.100 align:middle line:84%
It's a little tricky, but
actually, anything above p2

01:13:50.100 --> 01:13:51.058 align:middle line:90%
is going to work.

01:13:51.058 --> 01:13:52.600 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll write
it down as a set.

01:13:52.600 --> 01:13:55.380 align:middle line:84%
I mean, we're being a
little careful here.

01:13:55.380 --> 01:13:57.300 align:middle line:84%
If my opponent
charges p2, you're

01:13:57.300 --> 01:13:59.632 align:middle line:84%
exactly right that my
goal is to sell nothing

01:13:59.632 --> 01:14:01.340 align:middle line:84%
because the only way
I can sell something

01:14:01.340 --> 01:14:02.820 align:middle line:90%
is if I'm losing money.

01:14:02.820 --> 01:14:04.700 align:middle line:84%
How do I ensure
that I sell nothing?

01:14:04.700 --> 01:14:07.260 align:middle line:84%
Well, I don't even want to
charge p2 because then I'll

01:14:07.260 --> 01:14:08.857 align:middle line:90%
get half the market.

01:14:08.857 --> 01:14:10.440 align:middle line:84%
And then I'll lose
money on that half.

01:14:10.440 --> 01:14:14.140 align:middle line:84%
So it has to be strictly
above p2, so anything

01:14:14.140 --> 01:14:16.160 align:middle line:84%
strictly above P2
up to infinity.

01:14:16.160 --> 01:14:20.040 align:middle line:84%
So I'm using round parentheses
to denote strict inequalities.

01:14:20.040 --> 01:14:22.700 align:middle line:90%


01:14:22.700 --> 01:14:25.740 align:middle line:84%
Now, in particular, I mean, one
thing that's weird about this

01:14:25.740 --> 01:14:29.300 align:middle line:84%
is s might be willing to charge
a price below cost, which

01:14:29.300 --> 01:14:31.257 align:middle line:84%
seems really strange,
but I'm willing to do it

01:14:31.257 --> 01:14:33.340 align:middle line:84%
as long as the other
person's price is even lower,

01:14:33.340 --> 01:14:36.180 align:middle line:84%
and I'm not actually
going to sell.

01:14:36.180 --> 01:14:41.070 align:middle line:84%
What if my opponent
charges exactly c?

01:14:41.070 --> 01:14:42.130 align:middle line:90%
Now what do I--

01:14:42.130 --> 01:14:45.310 align:middle line:90%


01:14:45.310 --> 01:14:48.365 align:middle line:90%
what am I willing to charge?

01:14:48.365 --> 01:14:49.990 align:middle line:84%
Again, I'm not going
to be able to make

01:14:49.990 --> 01:14:53.470 align:middle line:84%
any money because the best I can
do is sell it at a price of c,

01:14:53.470 --> 01:14:54.550 align:middle line:90%
and my cost is c.

01:14:54.550 --> 01:14:56.410 align:middle line:84%
So I'm not going to
make a positive profit.

01:14:56.410 --> 01:14:59.110 align:middle line:84%
But I want to make sure I
don't make a negative profit.

01:14:59.110 --> 01:15:00.010 align:middle line:90%
So what should I do?

01:15:00.010 --> 01:15:00.787 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, over here.

01:15:00.787 --> 01:15:02.370 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Anywhere
from c to infinity.

01:15:02.370 --> 01:15:03.310 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly,
and in this case,

01:15:03.310 --> 01:15:05.365 align:middle line:84%
can I charge a price
of c exactly or not?

01:15:05.365 --> 01:15:05.990 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

01:15:05.990 --> 01:15:06.657 align:middle line:90%
IAN BALL: I can?

01:15:06.657 --> 01:15:08.870 align:middle line:84%
And why is that different
from this case here?

01:15:08.870 --> 01:15:11.830 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You charge [INAUDIBLE]
probability 0 at the same time.

01:15:11.830 --> 01:15:14.213 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: Exactly, so here
we have a closed parenthesis,

01:15:14.213 --> 01:15:15.130 align:middle line:90%
and it goes like this.

01:15:15.130 --> 01:15:16.050 align:middle line:90%
Notice the difference.

01:15:16.050 --> 01:15:18.470 align:middle line:84%
If I charge exactly the
same price as my opponent,

01:15:18.470 --> 01:15:20.270 align:middle line:90%
I get half the market.

01:15:20.270 --> 01:15:22.430 align:middle line:84%
If their price is
strictly below c, then

01:15:22.430 --> 01:15:25.030 align:middle line:84%
getting half the market means
I lose money because I'm losing

01:15:25.030 --> 01:15:26.990 align:middle line:90%
money on every unit I sell.

01:15:26.990 --> 01:15:28.790 align:middle line:84%
Here, getting half
the market gives me

01:15:28.790 --> 01:15:31.390 align:middle line:84%
a profit of exactly 0
because each unit I sell

01:15:31.390 --> 01:15:32.950 align:middle line:90%
is exactly at a price of c.

01:15:32.950 --> 01:15:37.222 align:middle line:90%


01:15:37.222 --> 01:15:42.470 align:middle line:84%
Now, OK, maybe to make
the cases a little easier,

01:15:42.470 --> 01:15:47.090 align:middle line:84%
let's say I'm just going to say
c is less than a 1/3 just to--

01:15:47.090 --> 01:15:49.590 align:middle line:84%
so I don't have to deal with
really complicated inequalities

01:15:49.590 --> 01:15:49.810 align:middle line:90%
here.

01:15:49.810 --> 01:15:51.435 align:middle line:84%
So let's just say c
is less than a 1/3.

01:15:51.435 --> 01:15:53.070 align:middle line:90%
It's not so important.

01:15:53.070 --> 01:15:57.110 align:middle line:84%
So now what happens if my
opponent charges strictly

01:15:57.110 --> 01:16:01.430 align:middle line:84%
more than c but less than or
equal to 1 minus c over 2?

01:16:01.430 --> 01:16:03.870 align:middle line:84%
The 1 minus c over
2 is not-- you'll

01:16:03.870 --> 01:16:07.870 align:middle line:90%
see later why that comes up.

01:16:07.870 --> 01:16:09.430 align:middle line:90%
No, 1 plus c over 2, sorry.

01:16:09.430 --> 01:16:15.790 align:middle line:90%


01:16:15.790 --> 01:16:18.227 align:middle line:90%
So actually, I don't need--

01:16:18.227 --> 01:16:19.810 align:middle line:84%
sorry, I don't need
c less than a 1/3.

01:16:19.810 --> 01:16:20.990 align:middle line:90%
Never mind, ignore that.

01:16:20.990 --> 01:16:22.750 align:middle line:84%
I made a mistake,
and then that caused

01:16:22.750 --> 01:16:24.708 align:middle line:84%
me to make another mistake
conditional on that.

01:16:24.708 --> 01:16:28.850 align:middle line:84%
So now my opponent's price
is strictly greater than c.

01:16:28.850 --> 01:16:30.210 align:middle line:90%
What price do I want to charge?

01:16:30.210 --> 01:16:37.602 align:middle line:90%


01:16:37.602 --> 01:16:39.060 align:middle line:84%
Well, they're
charging more than c,

01:16:39.060 --> 01:16:41.640 align:middle line:84%
so I can certainly
make positive profit.

01:16:41.640 --> 01:16:42.573 align:middle line:90%
But what's my goal?

01:16:42.573 --> 01:16:43.740 align:middle line:90%
What do I want to try to do?

01:16:43.740 --> 01:16:46.840 align:middle line:90%


01:16:46.840 --> 01:16:47.600 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:16:47.600 --> 01:16:50.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Charge as small
under p2 as possible.

01:16:50.260 --> 01:16:51.912 align:middle line:84%
IAN BALL: I want to
just undercut them.

01:16:51.912 --> 01:16:54.120 align:middle line:84%
And that creates a problem
here because, however much

01:16:54.120 --> 01:16:56.620 align:middle line:84%
undercut them, I could undercut
them by a little bit less.

01:16:56.620 --> 01:16:58.480 align:middle line:90%
Let's just say I charge $1 less.

01:16:58.480 --> 01:16:59.980 align:middle line:84%
Well, I'd rather
charge 1 cent less.

01:16:59.980 --> 01:17:01.540 align:middle line:84%
I'd rather charge
1/2 a cent less.

01:17:01.540 --> 01:17:04.060 align:middle line:84%
So it turns out,
technically, it's empty.

01:17:04.060 --> 01:17:06.220 align:middle line:84%
There's not a best response
because whatever I do,

01:17:06.220 --> 01:17:08.387 align:middle line:84%
I could do strictly better
because I could-- indeed,

01:17:08.387 --> 01:17:11.120 align:middle line:84%
my goal is to undercut them
by the smallest amount,

01:17:11.120 --> 01:17:13.080 align:middle line:84%
and I can't undercut
them by 0 because then I

01:17:13.080 --> 01:17:14.300 align:middle line:90%
only get half the market.

01:17:14.300 --> 01:17:15.860 align:middle line:84%
So I really want
to be strictly--

01:17:15.860 --> 01:17:16.818 align:middle line:90%
it's the classic thing.

01:17:16.818 --> 01:17:19.300 align:middle line:84%
What's the biggest number
that's strictly less than 1?

01:17:19.300 --> 01:17:20.548 align:middle line:90%
Well, it doesn't exist.

01:17:20.548 --> 01:17:22.840 align:middle line:84%
Now, as soon as I write this
down, I think you should--

01:17:22.840 --> 01:17:25.060 align:middle line:84%
some warning bells should go
off, and you should say, well,

01:17:25.060 --> 01:17:26.600 align:middle line:84%
is all this analysis
being driven

01:17:26.600 --> 01:17:29.640 align:middle line:84%
by some weird things about the
real numbers and limits not

01:17:29.640 --> 01:17:30.880 align:middle line:90%
existing?

01:17:30.880 --> 01:17:33.040 align:middle line:84%
Or is this really something
about the real world?

01:17:33.040 --> 01:17:34.560 align:middle line:84%
And you should ask,
well, what would

01:17:34.560 --> 01:17:37.320 align:middle line:84%
happen if we required prices
to be on a finite grid

01:17:37.320 --> 01:17:39.338 align:middle line:84%
and say prices have to
be denominated in cents?

01:17:39.338 --> 01:17:40.880 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out
that the conclusions

01:17:40.880 --> 01:17:42.080 align:middle line:90%
will be basically the same.

01:17:42.080 --> 01:17:45.200 align:middle line:84%
So whenever you have this
sinking feeling that there's

01:17:45.200 --> 01:17:47.540 align:middle line:84%
some weird math going on,
you should ask this question,

01:17:47.540 --> 01:17:48.220 align:middle line:90%
and people have.

01:17:48.220 --> 01:17:49.845 align:middle line:84%
And it turns out the
answer's the same,

01:17:49.845 --> 01:17:51.720 align:middle line:84%
so that's why we're
not too concerned.

01:17:51.720 --> 01:17:54.280 align:middle line:84%
OK, and then the
final case, this

01:17:54.280 --> 01:18:01.740 align:middle line:84%
is the trickiest one where p2 is
greater than one plus c over 2.

01:18:01.740 --> 01:18:05.000 align:middle line:90%
This is a bit tricky.

01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:08.220 align:middle line:84%
But what do I want
to do in this case?

01:18:08.220 --> 01:18:12.880 align:middle line:84%
You might think I want to
just undercut my opponent.

01:18:12.880 --> 01:18:16.680 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out that's
not the case here.

01:18:16.680 --> 01:18:19.500 align:middle line:84%
So let's recall in the monopoly
case where this demand function,

01:18:19.500 --> 01:18:22.520 align:middle line:84%
if I'm a monopolist, what is the
optimal price for me to charge

01:18:22.520 --> 01:18:24.880 align:middle line:90%
as a monopolist?

01:18:24.880 --> 01:18:28.263 align:middle line:84%
It turns out it's
exactly 1 plus c over 2.

01:18:28.263 --> 01:18:29.680 align:middle line:84%
If I were a
monopolist, that would

01:18:29.680 --> 01:18:32.250 align:middle line:90%
be the best price to charge.

01:18:32.250 --> 01:18:34.210 align:middle line:84%
So let's say my
opponent is charging

01:18:34.210 --> 01:18:36.210 align:middle line:90%
a ridiculously high price.

01:18:36.210 --> 01:18:38.690 align:middle line:84%
Well, then it's as
if I'm a monopolist.

01:18:38.690 --> 01:18:41.490 align:middle line:84%
And what I actually want
to do is charge exactly

01:18:41.490 --> 01:18:43.890 align:middle line:84%
the monopoly price
of 1 plus c over 2.

01:18:43.890 --> 01:18:49.490 align:middle line:90%


01:18:49.490 --> 01:18:51.570 align:middle line:84%
So notice where this
undercutting argument

01:18:51.570 --> 01:18:52.190 align:middle line:90%
breaks down.

01:18:52.190 --> 01:18:54.380 align:middle line:84%
You might think, oh, I
want to just undercut them.

01:18:54.380 --> 01:18:56.130 align:middle line:84%
But that is assuming
that they're charging

01:18:56.130 --> 01:18:57.510 align:middle line:90%
a pretty reasonable price.

01:18:57.510 --> 01:18:59.410 align:middle line:84%
If they're charging
such a high price

01:18:59.410 --> 01:19:02.170 align:middle line:84%
that if I just undercut them,
no one's going to buy from me,

01:19:02.170 --> 01:19:04.470 align:middle line:84%
then just undercutting
them no longer makes sense.

01:19:04.470 --> 01:19:07.830 align:middle line:84%
So if they're charging strictly
more than the monopoly price,

01:19:07.830 --> 01:19:09.890 align:middle line:84%
then I can just charge
the monopoly price.

01:19:09.890 --> 01:19:13.250 align:middle line:84%
I'm charging less than them,
so I get the whole market.

01:19:13.250 --> 01:19:15.490 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, I get the
highest possible profit

01:19:15.490 --> 01:19:17.770 align:middle line:84%
because I get exactly
the monopoly profit.

01:19:17.770 --> 01:19:20.670 align:middle line:84%
So here, you get
the monopoly profit.

01:19:20.670 --> 01:19:24.410 align:middle line:90%


01:19:24.410 --> 01:19:26.170 align:middle line:90%
So now we can work this all out.

01:19:26.170 --> 01:19:28.910 align:middle line:84%
We understand the best
response of firm 1 to firm 2.

01:19:28.910 --> 01:19:31.510 align:middle line:84%
We can compute firm 2's
best response to firm 1.

01:19:31.510 --> 01:19:33.230 align:middle line:84%
But it turns out, if
we do all the math,

01:19:33.230 --> 01:19:41.050 align:middle line:84%
there's a unique
equilibrium in which p1 star

01:19:41.050 --> 01:19:44.890 align:middle line:90%
equals P2 star equals c.

01:19:44.890 --> 01:19:47.010 align:middle line:90%
So we both charge exactly c.

01:19:47.010 --> 01:19:50.645 align:middle line:84%
And we can check, it's easy to
see that this is an equilibrium.

01:19:50.645 --> 01:19:52.270 align:middle line:84%
Showing it's unique
is a bit more work,

01:19:52.270 --> 01:19:56.050 align:middle line:84%
but seeing it's an equilibrium,
if my opponent charges c,

01:19:56.050 --> 01:19:58.290 align:middle line:90%
I'm willing to charge c.

01:19:58.290 --> 01:20:02.130 align:middle line:84%
And therefore, if we each charge
c, this is an equilibrium.

01:20:02.130 --> 01:20:04.490 align:middle line:84%
Now, notice a
fundamental difference

01:20:04.490 --> 01:20:06.290 align:middle line:90%
between Cournot and Bertrand.

01:20:06.290 --> 01:20:09.050 align:middle line:84%
With Cournot, each
time we added a firm,

01:20:09.050 --> 01:20:12.290 align:middle line:84%
the price got closer and closer
to the efficient price of c.

01:20:12.290 --> 01:20:14.330 align:middle line:84%
With Bertrand, it
only takes two.

01:20:14.330 --> 01:20:16.410 align:middle line:84%
As soon as we had
two firms competing,

01:20:16.410 --> 01:20:19.410 align:middle line:84%
this undercutting motive was
so strong that we immediately

01:20:19.410 --> 01:20:23.450 align:middle line:84%
got down to the efficient price
of price equals marginal cost.

01:20:23.450 --> 01:20:26.720 align:middle line:84%
All right, I'll end there, and
I'll see everyone on Thursday.

01:20:26.720 --> 01:20:30.000 align:middle line:90%